<node id="Carbon Dioxide">
  <data key="d0">Carbon Dioxide</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Carbon dioxide is a gaseous chemical compound that serves diverse roles across environmental science, industrial chemistry, and biological research. As a prominent greenhouse gas, its widespread emissions pose significant environmental threats, directly driving the development and deployment of carbon capture technologies. Notably, the PSC Technology has been shown to significantly reduce carbon dioxide emissions compared to conventional production methods. In industrial settings, carbon dioxide is frequently generated as a gaseous byproduct during several chemical processes, including the water-gas shift reaction within lignite gasification, the primary reaction involving calcium sulfate and coke, and the reduction of sulfate by carbon. In chemical notation, it is often represented as CO2↑ to indicate its gaseous state.

Beyond its environmental impact, carbon dioxide is actively utilized in material synthesis and mineral carbonation pathways. When dissolved in aqueous solutions, it reacts with Portlandite to form calcite, a property leveraged in mineral carbonation and material synthesis applications such as feldspar pellet processing. Additionally, carbon dioxide is captured from phosphogypsum decomposition slag and hydrolyzed liquid phases to synthesize high-quality calcium carbonate through crystallization and mineralization processes. During these operations, the gas is processed using hydrolysate derived from phosphogypsum decomposition, and its selectivity as a byproduct is frequently measured to evaluate the performance of specific catalysts, such as the PG-CuFe2O4 carrier.

In laboratory and biological systems, carbon dioxide functions as a precise reagent. It is routinely maintained at a 5% concentration in incubator atmospheres to support cell culture requirements. Furthermore, in specialized botanical or micro-environmental setups, it is supplied by plant hairs into micro-basins to facilitate specific chemical interactions. Collectively, these applications highlight carbon dioxide's versatility as both a critical environmental factor and a functional reagent across multiple scientific and industrial domains.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791209187</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deionized Water">
  <data key="d0">Deionized Water</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Deionized water is a purified liquid used as an input for the extraction and mineralization process.&lt;SEP&gt;Deionized water is an input used during the extraction and CO2 mineralization of milled feldspar pellets.&lt;SEP&gt;Deionized water is used to dilute digested samples and dissolve solids prior to filtration and analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Deionized Water is used to dilute dissolved particle samples prior to trace element analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Deionized Water is the liquid component mixed with powder components to form the homogeneous cement paste.&lt;SEP&gt;Deionized water is used as the liquid component to mix with powders for preparing the composite bone cement.&lt;SEP&gt;Deionized water was used to mix with compost samples for physicochemical analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791141872</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sulfur Dioxide">
  <data key="d0">Sulfur Dioxide</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sulfur dioxide is a gaseous byproduct released during the processing of feldspar pellets.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfur Dioxide is a gaseous byproduct generated during the thermal decomposition of phosphogypsum and the reaction between carbon and calcium sulfate.&lt;SEP&gt;A gas produced from phosphogypsum via thermodynamic and experimental processes for further chemical synthesis.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfur dioxide is a gaseous byproduct of the calcium sulfate and coke reaction, later processed in sulfuric acid production.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfur Dioxide is a pollutant whose emissions are significantly reduced by PSC Technology.&lt;SEP&gt;A gaseous product of the chemical reaction, represented as $2\text{SO}_2\uparrow$.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfur dioxide (SO2) is a gas released during the conversion of phosphogypsum and the reduction of sulfate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791209031</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nitrogen">
  <data key="d0">Nitrogen</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Nitrogen is a gaseous component released during the gasification of lignite.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen provides an inert atmosphere for the calcination step of feldspar pellet processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen is a gaseous component released during the gasification of lignite.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen is the inert gas atmosphere used during the calcination of feldspar pellets.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen is a gaseous component released during the gasification of lignite.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen is an essential plant nutrient solubilized by microorganisms and provided by the biofertilizer.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen is a chemical element listed in the data table with specified concentration values.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-046</data>
