<node id="Tunisia">
  <data key="d0">Tunisia</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Tunisia is a North African country situated along the Southern Mediterranean Sea and is the geographic location of the Gulf of Gabes. It serves as a primary industrial and scientific context for the study, characterization, and management of phosphogypsum. The nation hosts numerous phosphate fertilizer factories and coastal phosphate treatment plants, alongside significant phosphogypsum mining potential, establishing it as a recognized source of phosphogypsum samples in referenced literature. Within this industrial framework, the chemical compositions of phosphogypsum generated in Tunisia have been extensively analyzed and tabulated, with the country frequently appearing in analytical charts on the vertical axis corresponding to a weight percentage of approximately 42%. These factors collectively establish Tunisia as a key location for characterizing phosphate byproducts and assessing their environmental and managerial implications.

Environmentally, Tunisia is notable for the documented impacts of phosphate industry waste on the local marine ecosystem of the Gulf of Gabes. The country has also been the site of targeted investigations, including the examination of a phosphogypsum stack for groundwater contamination. These specific cases, alongside broader evaluations of management practices aimed at addressing environmental problems and developing required solutions, position Tunisia as a critical region for understanding the practical and technical challenges associated with phosphogypsum handling. Consequently, the nation remains a focal point for research into both the scientific characterization of phosphate byproducts and the ecological strategies needed to mitigate their industrial footprint.&lt;SEP&gt;Tunisia is a North African country where phosphogypsum piles are studied for their environmental impact on soil bacteria and wild plants.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791208013</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Southern Mediterranean Sea">
  <data key="d0">Southern Mediterranean Sea</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The Southern Mediterranean Sea is a geographical and marine region that encompasses and borders Gabes, Tunisia. It serves as the body of water directly adjacent to the coastal phosphate treatment plants located in this area.

This region has been the focus of environmental and scientific research, particularly concerning the ecological impacts of industrial activities. Studies, including those conducted by Zrelli, have analyzed the transport effects of phosphogypsum foam within these waters. Specifically, the Southern Mediterranean Sea has been examined as a marine environment where the movement and dispersion of metals and radionuclides, facilitated by phosphogypsum foam, are actively monitored and evaluated.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791119019</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Coastal Phosphate Treatment Plants">
  <data key="d0">Coastal Phosphate Treatment Plants</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Coastal phosphate treatment plants are industrial facilities in Tunisia that generate industrial wastes assessed for radiation hazards.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026</data>
