<node id="Rare Earth Elements">
  <data key="d0">Rare Earth Elements</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Rare Earth Elements (REEs) constitute a group of chemically similar, highly valuable, and strategically critical metallic and chemical elements that encompass the lanthanides, scandium, yttrium, cerium, lanthanum, and their associated oxides. Characterized primarily as ions with a +3 charge, these elements are indispensable to modern technology, functional materials, and daily life, with global demand projected to rise steadily, particularly between 2020 and 2025. Naturally occurring in phosphate rocks and mineral concentrates with geographically variable concentrations, REEs are also recognized in specific geological deposits, such as those in the Philippines. Their geological potential and extraction from waste materials are extensively analyzed in scientific literature.

Beyond primary natural deposits, Rare Earth Elements are prevalent as trace contaminants and impurities in various industrial byproducts and waste streams, including phosphoric acid sludge, WBS, TWBS, and notably phosphogypsum. Within phosphogypsum, they manifest as mineral inclusions, isomorphous substitutes, dispersed salts, or valuable components co-crystallized within the gypsum matrix. Despite their economic value, REEs are also identified as toxic and harmful components in phosphogypsum, alongside other metal trace elements, which necessitates careful handling and targeted management strategies.

The quantification and analysis of REEs in these waste streams are typically conducted using advanced analytical techniques such as X-ray fluorescence and inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS). To address environmental concerns while capitalizing on their economic potential, REEs are targeted for enhanced recovery through various chemical and physical processes. Extraction methods, often classified under broader utilization categories, employ advanced hydrometallurgical and separation techniques, including sulfuric acid leaching, resin-in-leach systems, sequential chemical processes, multi-step diffusion and bonding break procedures, and raw material beneficiation. These processes are similarly applied to the extraction and recycling of REEs from end-of-life applications and concentrates, with specific chemical equations describing their precipitation from phosphogypsum.

The recovery of Rare Earth Elements from phosphogypsum and related industrial residues serves multiple strategic objectives. It facilitates environmental remediation and pollution prevention, secures critical supply chains, and reduces reliance on primary natural resource extraction. Through targeted extraction, quantification, and enrichment, previously hazardous impurities are transformed into economically beneficial resources. These recovered REEs are subsequently utilized for a wide range of industrial applications, underscoring their substantial industrial value and strategic importance in sustainable resource management.&lt;SEP&gt;Rare Earth Elements represent a group of chemical elements including lanthanum, cerium, and neodymium, analyzed in the dihydrate phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Rare Earth Elements are a group of chemical elements that partition into phosphogypsum during phosphoric acid production.&lt;SEP&gt;A group of chemical elements that partition into phosphogypsum and phosphoric acid during production.&lt;SEP&gt;Rare earth elements are valuable minerals that can be leached from phosphogypsum using sulfuric acid.&lt;SEP&gt;Rare Earth Elements are valuable minerals recovered from PG through processes like sulfuric acid leaching.&lt;SEP&gt;Rare Earth Elements are valuable metallic components targeted for extraction and recovery from phosphogypsum waste streams.</data>
