<node id="Calcium Carbonate">
  <data key="d0">Calcium Carbonate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium Carbonate (CaCO3) is a chemical compound and salt that functions across both environmental and industrial domains. In soil systems, it is a fundamental component that actively participates in the dynamic equilibriums of the soil solution. Its solubility is typically quantified using the Calcium Carbonate Equivalent (CCE), and its behavior is highly responsive to ionic conditions; specifically, it deposits from the soil solution when calcium, bicarbonate, and carbonate ions are removed, and conversely dissolves back into the solution depending on fluctuations in carbonate ion concentration.

From an industrial and chemical processing perspective, Calcium Carbonate is generated through multiple synthesis and recovery pathways. A major production route involves processing phosphogypsum, where it is recovered as a co-product alongside calcium oxide during reductive decomposition, or alongside potassium sulfate during phosphogypsum conversion. It is also synthesized by capturing carbon dioxide into the alkaline solution or slag derived from phosphogypsum decomposition, followed by crystallization or recrystallization to yield a high-purity compound. This carbonation process frequently serves as the final product stage, notably forming at specific intervals such as the 74-minute mark. Additionally, Calcium Carbonate is produced using hydrolysate in industrial processes and can be recovered from slag generated after potassium extraction and CO2 mineralization from feldspar pellets. Due to its versatile properties and the efficiency of these resource-utilization and recycling methods, Calcium Carbonate is extensively utilized across a wide range of industrial applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium carbonate is a chemical compound that can be prepared from phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium Carbonate is a solid compound that undergoes dissolution and hydrolysis in water.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium carbonate removes fluoride and orthophosphate through precipitation and helps maintain high solution pH.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189785</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Carbonation Process">
  <data key="d0">Carbonation Process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Carbonation Process is a chemical transformation method that converts gypsum-derived compounds into calcium carbonate using CO2.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical transformation process that converts gypsum and Portlandite into calcium carbonate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790942862</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrolysis">
  <data key="d0">Hydrolysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydrolysis is a chemical reaction process fundamentally characterized by the interaction of water with various substances, leading to their breakdown, dissolution, or transformation into ions and other compounds. In its general mechanism, the process involves the cleavage of chemical bonds by water or hydroxide ions, often occurring concurrently with the dissolution of minerals such as calcium carbonate. This aqueous-driven transformation is essential for converting solid phases into soluble ionic species and other chemical compounds.

In specific industrial and materials science applications, hydrolysis is utilized across a range of distinct chemical systems. It is applied to calcium sulfide to produce an alkaline solution containing free calcium ions, hydrogen sulfide ions, and hydroxide ions, and is similarly employed to break down phosphogypsum decomposition slag into calcium sulfide and various impurities. The process also facilitates the aqueous dissolution of calcium-aluminum-silicate phases. Furthermore, hydrolysis plays a critical role in molecular bond cleavage, such as the breakdown of pyrophosphate bonds by hydroxide ions to yield water and phosphate ions, and is actively used in the synthesis of hydroxyapatite from precursor solutions. Collectively, these varied reactions demonstrate hydrolysis as a versatile chemical mechanism driven by aqueous interaction, essential for both material processing and compound synthesis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791120814</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CO2 Dissolution">
  <data key="d0">CO2 Dissolution</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">CO2 Dissolution is a process describing the equilibrium chain of CO2 transitioning between gas, aqueous, and carbonate forms.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790848098</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Precipitation">
  <data key="d0">Precipitation</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Precipitation is the chemical mechanism responsible for forming solid CaCO3 from aqueous ions.&lt;SEP&gt;Precipitation is a process where aluminum hydroxide forms as a solid, removing aluminum from the solution.&lt;SEP&gt;A mechanism for immobilizing potentially toxic elements in stabilized phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Precipitation is the process where dissolved ions solidify back into salt formations.&lt;SEP&gt;Precipitation is a separation technique used during hydrolysis to remove slightly soluble and insoluble impurities.&lt;SEP&gt;Precipitation is the primary mechanism for removing hydrogen fluoride from the atmosphere due to its high solubility.&lt;SEP&gt;Precipitation is the primary atmospheric removal mechanism for phosphogypsum and associated particulate matter.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791109370</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphate Industry">
  <data key="d0">Phosphate Industry</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphate Industry is the industrial sector that produces Raw PG, the initial feedstock for the valorization process.&lt;SEP&gt;An industrial sector that produces raw phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;An industrial sector whose waste products, such as phosphogypsum, are studied for environmental impact and resource recovery.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate industry is the sector associated with phosphate rock processing and byproduct generation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189670</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Washing Process">
  <data key="d0">Washing Process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Washing Process is a purification step that removes soluble impurities from Raw PG to produce Washed PG.&lt;SEP&gt;A purification step that removes soluble impurities from raw phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;The washing process removes sodium sulfate and facilitates further increases in rare earth element concentration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983741</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Carbonation System">
  <data key="d0">Carbonation System</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Carbonation System is the industrial apparatus where Portlandite reacts with CO2 to produce Calcite.&lt;SEP&gt;Industrial equipment where Portlandite reacts with CO2 to form calcite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
