<node id="Composting">
  <data key="d0">Composting</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Composting is a biological decomposition and soil treatment process primarily utilized to prepare phosphogypsum-based biofertilizers by combining the material with biomass and microbial agents. This preparation typically spans approximately 50 days and requires periodic turning. Throughout the process, the physicochemical environment is significantly altered, which in turn drives the evolution, succession, and structural stabilization of microbial communities. During this biological transformation, Actinobacteria consistently remain the dominant microbial group.

In addition to its role in microbial and physicochemical dynamics, composting functions as an effective soil treatment method that mitigates environmental and agricultural risks. By stabilizing the treated material, the process effectively reduces heavy metal transfer and limits cadmium penetration into plants, thereby enhancing soil safety and crop protection.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791140656</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="pH">
  <data key="d0">pH</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">pH is a fundamental physicochemical parameter that quantifies the acidity or alkalinity of substances, particularly within aqueous and soil environments. It serves as a critical baseline indicator across chemical, environmental, and biological research, and is routinely documented in chemical analysis data tables that detail substance composition. The measured values of pH fluctuate based on experimental and environmental conditions, typically ranging from 6.71 to 8.03, though they can be significantly reduced by phosphogypsum application or increased through interventions such as biofertilizer use. As a decisive regulatory variable, pH governs numerous geochemical and biological processes. It directly controls the solubility, mobility, and removal efficiency of various chemical species, including phosphorus, fluoride, orthophosphate, cadmium, lead, and thorium, while also dictating their subsequent plant uptake. Furthermore, pH influences bacterial activity, urease function, and the dominant chemical species present in various systems.

In industrial and waste management contexts, particularly concerning phosphogypsum, pH acts as the primary explanatory factor for chemical behavior, reactivity, and corrosiveness. It is systematically monitored within phosphogypsum stacks and during storage or composting operations, where it drives significant shifts in microbial community composition and dictates the stability of chemical precursors like REE-EDTA chelates. Daily pH tracking is essential during composting to identify and correct unsuitable conditions. Additionally, pH influences the behavior of chemical species in industrial runoff and serves as a key metric for assessing environmental impacts, such as the acidification of water and soil from phosphogypsum dumping, which adversely affects marine and terrestrial organisms. Collectively, these multifaceted roles establish pH as an indispensable variable across diverse scientific, agricultural, and industrial applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-022</data>
