<node id="Fly Ash">
  <data key="d0">Fly Ash</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Fly Ash is an industrial by-product and waste material primarily collected from power plants, widely utilized as a low-carbon supplementary cementitious material. Chemically, it is an aluminate-rich substance that actively promotes ettringite precipitation and pore filling within cemented paste backfill mixtures. Its mechanical properties are significantly enhanced when combined with phosphogypsum, establishing it as a frequent component in construction projects and hazardous waste containment systems.

In road engineering, Fly Ash is commonly mixed with lime to stabilize phosphogypsum for durable road material applications. Research also investigates its integration with phosphogypsum for environmental remediation, including efforts to mitigate greenhouse gas emissions from paddy fields. Furthermore, the material's performance under extreme conditions, such as elevated temperatures and thermal shock cycles, as well as its composite behavior under constant magnetic fields, has been extensively studied to optimize its industrial and construction applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Fly ash is an additive used with lime to stabilize phosphogypsum for road material applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Fly ash is a material studied under the effects of elevated temperatures and thermal shock cycles for construction applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Fly Ash is an industrial waste material combined with phosphogypsum to study composite properties under constant magnetic fields.&lt;SEP&gt;Fly ash is a combustion byproduct from coal burning that contains measurable baseline levels of radionuclides.&lt;SEP&gt;Fly ash is a raw material consumed in the PSC technology process.&lt;SEP&gt;A raw material consumed by the PSC process.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791209187</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gabes">
  <data key="d0">Gabes</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Gabes is a coastal city and region situated in southeastern Tunisia, widely recognized for its concentration of industrial phosphate treatment plants and phosphate fertilizer factories. The area is closely linked to significant industrial byproducts, most notably extensive phosphogypsum stockpiles and designated dumping sites. Due to these operations, the region has become a focal point for scientific assessments regarding industrial waste radioactivity and broader environmental impact.

Environmental and geochemical studies in Gabes frequently evaluate its water quality in comparison to the nearby island of Djerba. Research indicates notably lower pH values in waters adjacent to phosphogypsum disposal zones. Comprehensive scientific characterizations of the region document a water pH range spanning 6.2 to 7.9, alongside distinct marine and geological features such as seafloor nodules and a high abundance of siliceous particles. These combined factors underscore Gabes as a major industrial center where economic activity intersects with ongoing environmental monitoring and geochemical research.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791118833</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Springer International Publishing">
