<node id="CaS">
  <data key="d0">CaS</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">CaS (calcium sulfide) is a chemical compound characterized by distinct morphological traits and diverse applications across material science and industrial chemistry. In composite materials, it is known to form needle-like, interlocked crystals. Within the specific context of composite bone cements, CaS functions as a primary component and is synthesized alongside HAP to establish the base matrix. It is specifically identified as a calcium sulfide phase within the x = 0 sample composition of these bone cements.

Beyond biomedical applications, CaS plays a significant role in various chemical processing and reaction studies. It actively participates in oxidation and solid-solid reactions, and its formation is notably constrained by the presence of iron (Fe) and nickel (Ni). The compound is generated as a reduction product during specific procedural stages, namely Steps 3 and 5. Additionally, CaS is utilized in experimental setups involving phosphogypsum, where it facilitates the investigation of melting characteristics. Collectively, these attributes highlight CaS as a multifunctional compound integral to both advanced composite formulations and specialized chemical reaction analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;CaS is a chemical compound involved in reactions with phosphogypsum to study melting characteristics.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-025</data>
