<node id="Acid Leaching">
  <data key="d0">Acid Leaching</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Acid Leaching is a hydrometallurgical and chemical separation technique utilized to extract valuable elements, particularly rare earth elements, rare earth metals, and lanthanides, from solid materials. The process functions as a chemical decomposition and extraction method that employs acidic solutions, specifically mineral acids such as HCl, HNO3, and H2SO4. By introducing these acids to the target material, the technique dissolves the underlying matrix, a mechanism that is frequently represented and analyzed through chemical reaction equations.

The primary application of Acid Leaching involves recovering critical metals from industrial waste and solid byproducts, with extensive research focused on phosphogypsum and phosphohemihydrate. In these specific contexts, the process targets the gypsum matrix to break it down and release soluble rare earth elements into an aqueous solution. Beyond industrial waste, the technique is also applicable to raw ores, serving as a versatile hydrometallurgical approach for converting solid-bound valuable elements into a liquid form for subsequent recovery and processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Acid leaching is a process investigated for extracting rare earth elements from phosphogypsum using acids such as HCl, HNO3, and H2SO4.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-023</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
