<node id="Phosphate">
  <data key="d0">Phosphate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A chemical compound that reacts with sulfuric acid in the described process.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate is a chemical compound found in phosphate ores and phosphogypsum, with low bioavailability due to fixation with metals.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate is a primary chemical component of phosphogypsum and an essential nutrient for plant growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate is a chemical component in phosphogypsum that influences the properties of cemented backfill.&lt;SEP&gt;A phosphate ion shown with a central purple sphere bonded to four red spheres, present in starting materials and released as a final ion.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate is the anionic group in apatite that can be substituted by carbonate, vanadate, arsenate, and sulfate.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate is a chemical compound found at high concentrations in groundwater near phosphogypsum stacks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002</data>
