<node id="Thorium">
  <data key="d0">Thorium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Thorium is a chemical element and natural radionuclide that occurs in the +4 oxidation state, primarily migrating in a polymeric colloidal form and converting into a soluble sulfate form. As a trace element, it is naturally present in phosphate rocks, phosphorites, and marketable rock phosphate. Its concentration in phosphorites is comparable to that in shales, contributing to the natural radioactivity of these geological formations.

During industrial acid processing, particularly sulfuric acid processing and the wet phosphoric acid process, thorium becomes mobilized and distributes primarily into the phosphoric acid stream, while simultaneously accumulating in the phosphogypsum byproduct. The concentration of thorium (C_Th) is measured by particle size and is utilized in Ra-Eq calculations. Given its radioactive properties and tendency to accumulate in phosphogypsum during sulfuric acid processing, thorium requires careful management and handling protocols.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791187056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sulfate">
  <data key="d0">Sulfate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sulfate is a double-charged chemical anion that functions as a discrete ion or integral component of broader compound groups, such as thorium(IV) sulfate. In aqueous environments, it predominantly exists in a free state, and its molar fraction is routinely calculated using the solution's effective ionic strength. Chemically, sulfate is highly reactive, frequently appearing in initial chemical equations where it drives precipitation or extraction processes. It acts as a reactive anion in speciation modeling, notably interacting with cadmium, while also substituting for phosphate within apatite crystal structures. Furthermore, sulfate enhances the partitioning of uranium into gypsum crystal lattices and forms sludges with radium and barium, directly influencing radium solubility.

Industrially and environmentally, sulfate serves as a primary and dominant constituent of phosphogypsum and gypsum stacks. It migrates from these stacks into surrounding environmental compartments and is a key component of process water and leachate. The environmental fate of sulfate in phosphogypsum leachate is commonly predicted using GEOCHEM modeling software. Monitoring across experimental and environmental stages consistently tracks sulfate concentrations, which are routinely cataloged in analytical data tables, often denoted with specific superscript notation, and typically exhibit a steady downward trend, though levels can increase during neutralization processes. Groundwater measurements frequently reveal sulfate concentrations significantly elevated above natural background levels.

Remediation and water quality management of sulfate are well-documented. It can be effectively removed from process water and leachate through interaction with geological materials, with removal efficiency notably enhanced by the presence of iron(III) hydroxide and abundant calcium. Due to its prevalence and potential impacts, sulfate is subject to established regulatory thresholds, including a general limit of 250 mg/L and a World Health Organization guideline value of 25 mg/L. Collectively, these characteristics underscore sulfate's significance as both a fundamental reactive chemical species and a quantitatively monitored environmental parameter.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfate is a compound reduced by carbon, resulting in the release of sulfur dioxide and carbon dioxide gases.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
