<node id="China">
  <data key="d0">China</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">China is a major global industrial and environmental entity that frequently serves as the geographic, regulatory, and research focus for numerous academic and policy studies. As the world’s largest producer of phosphogypsum, generating approximately 80 million tons annually alongside substantial phosphate fertilizer output, the nation faces significant accumulation challenges. Despite strict governmental regulations, extensive recycling surveys, and historical research initiatives dating back to the 1950s for gypsum decomposition into sulfuric acid and cement, a considerable portion of this byproduct remains emitted or stockpiled. To bridge this gap, China has implemented comprehensive policies targeting a 75% comprehensive utilization rate for phosphogypsum by 2025. Contemporary strategies actively explore leveraging gypsum waste to treat industrial carbon dioxide-containing tail gas, facilitating resource regeneration. Consequently, the country remains the primary geographic region for evaluating phosphogypsum disposal practices, utilization technologies, lifecycle inventory analyses, and the environmental performance of Plaster of Paris applications.

Beyond phosphogypsum management, China plays a pivotal role in critical minerals and climate policy. The nation hosts major rare earth deposits, notably at Bayan Obo and Longnan, which serve as focal points for life cycle assessment case studies on rare earth recovery. Historically navigating international scrutiny over its supply monopoly, China has gradually reduced rare earth export quotas. In the climate domain, China stands as the world’s largest carbon dioxide emitter and has enacted comprehensive policies to achieve carbon neutrality. These initiatives actively address the challenges and opportunities of the energy transition while promoting broader adoption of energy-saving measures. Additionally, China is the world’s most productive country regarding straw production and maintains a strong domestic demand for building gypsum, all within a regulatory framework that enforces strict solid waste management standards.

In analytical and research contexts, China is extensively referenced across studies evaluating industrial resources, environmental protection, and academic methodologies. It is frequently positioned with a substantial quantitative influence, often carrying a weight percentage of approximately 44% in relevant analytical frameworks. The nation’s industrial scale, environmental challenges, and policy innovations continue to drive global research on resource utilization, waste management, and sustainable development.</data>
