<node id="Leaching">
  <data key="d0">Leaching</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Leaching is a chemical and experimental extraction process primarily employed to recover rare earth elements and associated impurities from phosphogypsum or other solid waste matrices. In industrial resource recovery applications, the technique utilizes dilute acids or various other reagents to establish favorable chemical conditions that facilitate the migration and separation of rare earth elements from the solid phosphogypsum matrix into a liquid solution. This process is frequently applied to dried phosphogypsum and serves as a critical step in material recovery; however, it also extracts impurities that may escape during subsequent resource product generation, thereby introducing potential operational and environmental risks.

In a distinct environmental context, leaching refers to a natural geochemical transport mechanism wherein dissolved substances, such as fluorine, migrate downward from the ground into underlying water bodies. While the industrial application focuses on controlled chemical extraction for resource recovery, the environmental definition highlights the natural downward movement of elements through soil and groundwater systems. Together, these perspectives encompass leaching as both a targeted extraction methodology and a natural subsurface transport process.&lt;SEP&gt;Leaching is a process where acids are used to create favorable conditions for migrating rare earth elements from solid waste to a solution.&lt;SEP&gt;Leaching is a chemical extraction process used to recover rare earth elements and impurities from phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Leaching is the process of washing soluble substances like sodium out of the soil, promoted by increased water infiltration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Leaching Solution">
  <data key="d0">Leaching Solution</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The leaching solution is formed through long-term precipitation and surface runoff, carrying and enriching rare earth elements and heavy metals.&lt;SEP&gt;The leaching solution is formed through long-term precipitation and surface runoff, carrying and enriching rare earth elements and heavy metals.&lt;SEP&gt;The leaching solution is formed through long-term precipitation and surface runoff, carrying and enriching rare earth elements and heavy metals.</data>
