<node id="Carbon">
  <data key="d0">Carbon</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Carbon, as a fundamental chemical element denoted by the symbol C, serves as an essential component in various natural and industrial processes. It is a major constituent found in phosphogypsum samples, with measured mass fractions typically ranging between 0.030% and 0.230%. In biological systems, carbon functions as a crucial element that is actively cycled by Actinobacteria and other soil microorganisms. Chemically, it frequently acts as a reducing agent in high-temperature reduction reactions and in processes involving sulfate, where it facilitates chemical transformations under specific conditions.

In industrial and material processing contexts, carbon also refers to a distinct solid product generated during the initial decomposition of lignite. This specific material exhibits notable chemical reactivity, particularly reacting slowly with calcium sulfate under reducing atmospheres during the traditional decomposition of phosphogypsum. The dual usage of the term highlights how carbon encompasses both a ubiquitous chemical element vital to ecological cycles and a specific solid substance produced through thermal decomposition in industrial applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791209031</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Electrodialysis">
  <data key="d0">Electrodialysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Electrodialysis is a recycling process combined with bipolar membranes to improve the sustainability of phosphogypsum processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrodialysis is a separation technique integrated with bipolar membranes to enhance the sustainability of phosphogypsum recycling.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrodialysis is a recycling process combined with bipolar membranes to improve the sustainability of phosphogypsum processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrodialysis is a separation technique integrated with bipolar membranes to enhance the sustainability of phosphogypsum recycling.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrodialysis is a recycling process combined with bipolar membranes to improve the sustainability of phosphogypsum processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrodialysis is a separation technique integrated with bipolar membranes to enhance the sustainability of phosphogypsum recycling.</data>
