<node id="Fig. 5">
  <data key="d0">Fig. 5</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Fig. 5 is a generic figure label utilized across multiple scientific publications, with its specific content varying significantly depending on the source document. In the context of phosphogypsum (PG) research, Fig. 5 illustrates the resource utilization mechanism of PG, as well as the process or mechanism for generating syngas using PG within the CLG process. Another study employing this label focuses on cadmium (Cd) behavior following PG application, specifically displaying the percentage of the mole fraction of water-soluble Cd in water extracts and illustrating the distribution of Cd content across various associated ion forms derived from model calculations.

In investigations involving rare earth elements, Fig. 5 presents elemental mapping images and spot characterizations that reveal rare earth element occurrences in acid-leached phosphogypsum. Additionally, it depicts the area outlines of rare earth element mineral inclusions within leaching residue and highlights their spatial overlap with cerium distribution. Regarding materials science and cementitious composites, Fig. 5 displays scanning electron microscopy (SEM) micrographs of the fracture surfaces of composite cements, with specific instances detailing composite bone cement fracture surfaces across compositions ranging from x = 0 to 2.5 wt%.

Beyond materials and chemical processes, Fig. 5 is also used to present a single factor analysis of sample differences alongside microflora networks. Due to the generic nature of the label, some instances merely reference Fig. 5 within the text without providing a detailed description of its specific content. Collectively, these descriptions confirm that Fig. 5 represents distinct figures from different studies or sections, each tailored to its respective scientific focus, whether it involves phosphogypsum utilization, heavy metal speciation, rare earth element mapping, composite cement microstructure, or microbial network analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 5 is a figure illustrating the environmental impact of main indicators and economic impact for 1t PG resource-utilization under varying scenarios.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791208637</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fig. 6">
  <data key="d0">Fig. 6</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Fig. 6 serves as a descriptive figure across multiple distinct scientific contexts. In environmental and materials chemistry research concerning phosphogypsum (PG), the figure illustrates the solidification and stabilization of toxic impurities within the material. It specifically depicts the concentration and chemical forms of cadmium present in water extracts from soil following phosphogypsum application. Furthermore, it displays elemental mapping images that compare rare earth elements, particularly cerium, with calcium in leached phosphogypsum. These comparative maps indicate the release of embedded elements from the gypsum lattice, visually documenting elemental distribution and mobility during leaching processes.

In a separate biomedical engineering context, Fig. 6 presents scanning electron microscopy (SEM) micrographs of composite bone cement fracture surfaces at higher magnification. These high-resolution images are utilized to illustrate the porosity levels evident on the fracture surfaces, providing detailed visual evidence of the internal microstructure and void distribution within the cement composite.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 6 illustrates the solidification and stabilization of toxic impurities in PG.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 6 displays functional prediction results and abundance differences in bacterial communities.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 6 illustrates the environmental and economic impact of China's PG disposal in 2021 and potential changes under optimized scenarios.</data>
