<node id="Uranium">
  <data key="d0">Uranium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Uranium is a naturally occurring radioactive chemical element and radionuclide that serves as a primary contributor to the natural radioactivity of phosphorites and phosphate rocks. Chemically, it is a polyvalent element whose partitioning, dissolution, and complex solubility relationships are heavily influenced by redox conditions and specific industrial processing methods. During the wet phosphoric acid production process, uranium predominantly distributes into the phosphoric acid stream. It is systematically extracted from phosphate rocks and frequently accumulates as a trace element in industrial byproducts such as phosphogypsum.

In environmental and geochemical contexts, uranium migration and presence are well-documented across phosphate materials, groundwater, mine waste waters, and soil-to-produce transfer pathways. Following the disposal of phosphogypsum, uranium concentrations have been observed to increase in marine ecosystems, highlighting the necessity for effective recovery strategies to prevent long-term ecological contamination. Its presence in phosphogypsum waste and mining environments poses significant radiation hazards, necessitating rigorous monitoring. Researchers routinely quantify uranium in phosphogypsum samples, measuring micrograms per gram across various grain fractions, and systematically separate and purify it for precise alpha spectrometry analysis. Additionally, uranium is routinely analyzed in air samples collected near phosphogypsum ponds.

From an occupational and public health perspective, uranium functions as both a critical analyte for radiometric assessment and a key environmental contaminant requiring careful management. Occupational safety protocols mandate the continuous monitoring of cumulative internal radiation doses for workers stationed near phosphogypsum waste piles and active mine sites. Together, these factors underscore uranium's dual significance as a valuable component of phosphate processing, a subject of extensive geochemical and radiometric study, and a persistent environmental hazard that demands comprehensive monitoring and mitigation across ecological, occupational, and industrial domains.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012</data>
