<node id="Phosphogypsum (PG)">
  <data key="d0">Phosphogypsum (PG)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphogypsum (PG) is an industrial byproduct and waste residue generated during the production of phosphate fertilizers. It typically manifests as a finely granulated powder, silt, or silty-sand material. Chemically, PG is primarily composed of calcium sulfate dihydrate alongside silica or quartz, and contains high concentrations of calcium ions mixed with various impurities. Because of its specific composition and morphology, its resource utilization is often restricted, prompting extensive physico-chemical characterization and elemental analysis to better evaluate its properties and potential applications.

Despite these utilization constraints, PG serves as a valuable matrix material with several industrial applications. It acts as a primary source for the recovery of rare earth elements and is utilized as an oxygen carrier in gasification processes. Furthermore, the material exhibits notable chemical reactivity; its high calcium ion content enables it to react with atmospheric carbon dioxide, ultimately forming stable calcite.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum (PG) is a byproduct of the phosphate fertilizer industry that poses environmental challenges due to high production and complex composition.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum is an industrial byproduct primarily composed of calcium sulfate, targeted for recycling and resource utilization in China.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum (PG) is an industrial byproduct utilized as a raw material in cement retarder and building gypsum production.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum (PG) is an industrial byproduct from phosphate fertilizer production, serving as the primary subject of resource utilization analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791207188</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Silica">
  <data key="d0">Silica</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Silica is a primary component of phosphogypsum, accounting for over 90 percent of its composition alongside calcium sulfate dihydrate.&lt;SEP&gt;Silica is a primary component of phosphogypsum, accounting for over 90 percent of its composition alongside calcium sulfate dihydrate.&lt;SEP&gt;Silica is an impurity present in phosphogypsum that participates in the formation of aluminosilicate phases.&lt;SEP&gt;Silica is a chemical used in combination with sulphuric acid to treat phosphogypsum and reduce impurities.&lt;SEP&gt;Silica is a primary component of phosphogypsum, accounting for over 90 percent of its composition alongside calcium sulfate dihydrate.&lt;SEP&gt;Silica is an impurity in phosphogypsum that may be added during production or originate from process water supplies.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
