<node id="Magnesium">
  <data key="d0">Magnesium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Magnesium is a double-charged chemical ion that is analyzed in both solution chemistry and industrial material contexts. In aqueous solutions, its molar fraction is calculated based on effective ionic strength, and its concentration levels are systematically tracked across experimental stages in accompanying charts and tables. During these experiments, magnesium exhibits a concentration trend that closely mirrors calcium, marked by a significant drop at the 20-stage mark.

In phosphogypsum, magnesium is identified as both an impurity and a measurable major element. It is present in phosphogypsum samples with mass fractions ranging from 0.040% to 1.500%. Despite this quantifiable presence, its relative mass fraction remains lower when compared to other primary elements within the material. These combined characteristics highlight magnesium's dual relevance in experimental solution tracking and its specific geochemical presence in phosphogypsum matrices.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnesium is an impurity element present in phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791120500</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Aluminum">
  <data key="d0">Aluminum</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Aluminum is a chemical element and metal that is extensively monitored in environmental and industrial contexts, particularly within phosphogypsum and its associated process water. In phosphogypsum, aluminum functions as both a major constituent and an impurity element. Its concentration is routinely measured and reported across multiple scientific studies and datasets, with mass fractions in phosphogypsum samples typically ranging from 0.090% to 1.920%. When present in phosphogypsum process water, aluminum is tracked as a key chemical parameter, with reported concentrations varying depending on the specific source.

Beyond its presence in phosphogypsum systems, aluminum exhibits significant chemical interactions in agricultural and thermal processing environments. In soil, it acts as a binding agent for phosphate, precipitating the nutrient into insoluble forms and thereby reducing its bioavailability and accessibility to plants. Additionally, aluminum serves a functional role in industrial applications, where it acts as a metal component within phosphogypsum that helps lower the decomposition temperature during gasification. These combined characteristics underscore aluminum's diverse chemical behavior and its relevance across environmental monitoring, soil chemistry, and material processing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-019</data>
