<node id="Arsenic">
  <data key="d0">Arsenic</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Arsenic is a toxic and harmful element that is frequently characterized as a heavy metal in environmental and agricultural literature. It commonly occurs as a trace component in phosphogypsum, with measured mass contents typically ranging from 2.00 to 4.50 μg/g. The presence of arsenic in this material presents considerable environmental and public health risks, primarily due to its tendency to accumulate in soil and be readily absorbed by various crops. This bioaccumulation pathway introduces significant food safety concerns, as the element can enter the human food chain through contaminated agricultural produce.

To mitigate these risks, specific agricultural and regulatory measures have been implemented. For instance, the application of sprinkler irrigation has been demonstrated to effectively lower arsenic concentrations in rice grains. Furthermore, recognizing its potential to contaminate water resources and affect crop cultivation, arsenic is strictly regulated within water quality standards. These regulations establish precise concentration limits for various uses, including dry crop irrigation, ensuring that exposure remains within safe thresholds. Through combined monitoring, agricultural adaptation, and regulatory oversight, the management of arsenic aims to balance industrial byproduct utilization with the protection of ecological and human health.&lt;SEP&gt;Arsenic is a toxic and harmful element found in phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Arsenic is a trace element enriched in phosphorites relative to crustal abundances, often associated with organic matter.&lt;SEP&gt;Arsenic is a trace element whose concentration did not decrease greatly with pH increases and poses an environmental risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Arsenic is a chemical parameter measured in phosphogypsum process water, listed in the reported values table.&lt;SEP&gt;Arsenic is a toxic chemical element measured in phosphogypsum samples across multiple scientific studies.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical element with a specified limit of 0.05 mg l⁻¹ across multiple regulatory frameworks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-019</data>
