  <data key="d0">Chromium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Chromium is a heavy metal and trace element characterized by its toxic and harmful properties, which pose significant environmental and health risks. It is commonly found in phosphogypsum, where it substantially contributes to the material's toxicological profile. Analytical measurements of phosphogypsum samples indicate that chromium mass contents typically range from 12.99 to 30.00 μg/g. As a heavy metal ion, chromium readily leaches from phosphogypsum and migrates through various environmental compartments, facilitating its dispersion across different ecosystems.

Beyond phosphogypsum, chromium contamination has been documented in stockyard soils, with concentrations frequently exceeding the GB 15618-2018 risk control standard. Due to its mobility and potential for ecological impact, chromium is actively monitored and classified within surface water and irrigation water frameworks. These regulatory and monitoring efforts are designed to track its environmental trajectory and mitigate adverse effects on both public health and aquatic systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromium is a toxic and harmful element found in phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromium is a trace element enriched in phosphorites relative to crustal abundances.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical element with a limit of 0.05 mg l⁻¹ and a transfer rate of 3–26 percent in phosphogypsum processing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-047</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791186664</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cadmium">
  <data key="d0">Cadmium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Cadmium is a toxic heavy metal and trace chemical element that functions as a significant environmental pollutant and contaminant, posing severe risks to plant life, agricultural ecosystems, broader trophic chains, and human health. It is notably concentrated in phosphogypsum and phosphorites, where it is significantly enriched relative to shale and often substitutes for calcium in apatite. Investigations of phosphogypsum dumping sites frequently detect abnormal concentrations, with measured mass contents in samples ranging from 0.04 to 1.48 μg/g, and notably high levels identified in Tunisian deposits. Due to its persistence and mobility, cadmium accumulates in agricultural soils primarily through fertilizer application and various technogenic sources, making it a critical focus for environmental monitoring and ecological risk assessment.

The environmental behavior of cadmium is heavily governed by chemical equilibria, which dictate its mobility, bioavailability, and chemical speciation in soil and water systems. Scientific modeling demonstrates that its equilibrium concentration in soil solutions typically exerts a minimal impact on overall ionic strength, while researchers utilize mathematical equations to analyze its water-soluble forms, adsorption properties, and speciation. In phosphogypsum processing, cadmium exhibits a transfer rate of 30–54 percent, and its concentration in pore water decreases significantly as pH increases. These complex soil-water interactions substantially influence agricultural productivity and ecological health.

Agricultural practices play a pivotal role in cadmium uptake by crops; for example, sprinkler irrigation has been shown to enhance its concentration in rice grains. Conversely, high application rates of phosphogypsum do not necessarily increase cadmium uptake in crops, highlighting the nuanced relationship between soil amendment and metal bioavailability. Given its toxicity, cadmium is subject to stringent regulatory controls, including a water quality limit of 0.01 mg l⁻¹ and classification as a high contaminant under Quebec standards. Collectively, these characteristics underscore cadmium's complex environmental fate and its substantial impact on ecological and agricultural systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-033</data>
