<node id="Fluoride">
  <data key="d0">Fluoride</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Fluoride is a chemical species derived from fluorine that functions as both an element and a compound across environmental, industrial, and agricultural contexts. Primarily originating from phosphate rock, it is a pervasive impurity found in phosphogypsum in both soluble and insoluble forms, as well as in process water, leachate, and aqueous solutions. Due to its diffusive mobility in soil and high bio-accumulative potential, fluoride readily disperses through ecosystems, often adsorbing in topsoil while accumulating in plants and entering the food chain. In groundwater and leachate, its concentrations frequently exceed background levels and drinking water standards, with detections reaching up to 32 mg/L against a regulatory limit of 4.0 mg/L and a World Health Organization guideline of 1.5 mg/L.

As a toxic contaminant, fluoride poses significant ecological and health risks. It causes fluorosis in livestock and respiratory issues in humans when inhaled or ingested. Industrial processes can release it as gaseous hydrogen fluoride and silicon tetrafluoride, contributing to atmospheric contamination. Under specific geochemical conditions, such as low pH and reduced environments, fluoride ions can complex with uranous ions. Its environmental fate in phosphogypsum leachate has been modeled using the GEOCHEM software. The presence of fluoride raises substantial concerns regarding the application of phosphogypsum as a soil amendment.

Remediation and management efforts focus on immobilizing and removing fluoride from aqueous and soil environments. Advanced treatment strategies employ phosphogypsum-derived hydroxyapatite nanoparticles to extract the contaminant from water, while chemical precipitation methods form stable compounds like fluorite, aluminum fluoride complexes, and calcium fluorophosphate. Additionally, geological materials are utilized for its removal. These combined scientific and engineering approaches aim to mitigate fluoride's environmental mobility, reduce its toxicity, and ensure agricultural and ecological safety.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluoride is an air emission listed in the emissions table.&lt;SEP&gt;An emission produced by PBG and PSC processes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-004</data>
