</node>
<node id="Silicon Dioxide">
  <data key="d0">Silicon Dioxide</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Silicon dioxide (SiO₂) is a chemical compound commonly found in vitreous glass, quartz, and phosphogypsum. It is readily identified through X-ray diffraction (XRD) analysis, which typically reveals characteristic diffraction peaks, including a specific peak labeled Q at approximately 20 degrees. Additionally, the compound exhibits distinct chemical reactivity, making it a frequent target for hydrofluoric acid-based etching processes.

In industrial applications involving phosphogypsum, silicon dioxide acts as an impurity that notably improves sintering resistance when the material is utilized as an oxygen carrier. Conversely, during beneficiation operations, it is treated as a major component that must be removed. Consequently, silicon dioxide is systematically eliminated from washed beneficiated concentrates through high concentration and agitation processing techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;Silicon Dioxide is a chemical compound whose concentration is measured and reported in the dataset.&lt;SEP&gt;Silicon Dioxide is a chemical compound listed in the data table with specified concentration ranges.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791188093</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphorus Pentoxide">
  <data key="d0">Phosphorus Pentoxide</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphorus pentoxide is a chemical compound intrinsically linked to phosphogypsum, where it exists as a residual impurity and is entrained within the material's composition. During the industrial production of phosphogypsum, this compound is typically extracted as part of the processing workflow. Depending on the specific chemical treatments applied, phosphorus pentoxide can be recovered alongside rare earth elements during sulfuric acid processing, or its presence can be actively reduced through citric acid treatment.

In analytical and industrial record-keeping, phosphorus pentoxide is formally tracked as a key chemical component. Its concentration is routinely measured and reported within standardized datasets. When cataloged in data tables, the compound is explicitly listed and often accompanied by specific superscript notation to denote precise analytical values or experimental conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791188249</data>
