<node id="Calcium Ion">
  <data key="d0">Calcium Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Calcium Ion (Ca²⁺) is a positively charged chemical species and exchangeable macro-ion that exists as a free ion across various environmental and experimental settings. Its concentration is routinely measured to monitor chemical dynamics, and it serves as a versatile participant in both geological and biochemical processes.

In soil and geological contexts, the Calcium Ion acts as a key exchangeable cation within Haplic Chernozem soils. It resides in the soil solution as a macro-ion that forms associates and actively participates in carbonate deposition. It is also a primary constituent abundant in gypsum, where it contributes to the mineral's geochemical stability and reactivity.

Chemically and biologically, the Calcium Ion readily precipitates with phosphate ions to form insoluble calcium phosphate. In simulated body fluid (SBF) systems, it binds to negative surface charges on hydroxyapatite to nucleate calcium-rich amorphous calcium phosphate, a mechanism vital for biomimetic mineralization. Additionally, its chemical properties enable it to facilitate fluoride removal in aqueous solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium Ion is a positively charged cation released into solution during calcium carbonate dissolution.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium ions are structural components of the gypsum crystal lattice that can be substituted by uranyl ions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Magnesium Ion">
  <data key="d0">Magnesium Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Magnesium ion is an exchangeable cation present in the Haplic Chernozem soil.&lt;SEP&gt;The Magnesium Ion (Mg^2+) is a macro-ion present in the soil solution that forms associates.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnesium Ion (Mg²⁺) is a chemical species whose concentration was measured across the experimental conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnesium ion is an exchangeable cation present in the Haplic Chernozem soil.&lt;SEP&gt;The Magnesium Ion (Mg^2+) is a macro-ion present in the soil solution that forms associates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790951494</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sulfate Ion">
  <data key="d0">Sulfate Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Sulfate Ion (SO₄²⁻) is an inorganic sulfur compound and anionic macro-ion that functions across diverse environmental and chemical systems. In soil environments, it exists as a macro-ion within the soil solution where it readily forms associates, and its concentration is routinely quantified across various experimental conditions. Chemically, it acts as an anion that actively participates in cadmium complexation within aqueous solutions.

In industrial and atmospheric contexts, particularly regarding phosphogypsum, the sulfate ion is identified via infrared spectroscopy and serves as a characteristic pollutant in leachate generated from phosphogypsum stockpiles. It also exhibits strong hygroscopic behavior by competing for moisture in the atmosphere, notably with hydrogen fluoride. This competitive interaction significantly prolongs the atmospheric residence time of phosphogypsum and associated atmospheric mixtures.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfate ion is a negatively charged chemical species present as a reactant in the chemical equation and as a spectator ion in the stabilization process.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791119375</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Carbonate Ion">
  <data key="d0">Carbonate Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Carbonate Ion (CO3^2-) is a macro-ion present in the soil solution that forms associates and participates in calcium carbonate deposition.&lt;SEP&gt;An ion ($\mathrm{CO}_3^{2-}$) present in soil solution that influences calcium carbonate deposition and dissolution.&lt;SEP&gt;The Carbonate Ion is a chemical species whose concentration is measured alongside bicarbonate in the experimental data.&lt;SEP&gt;The Carbonate Ion (CO3^2-) is a macro-ion present in the soil solution that forms associates and participates in calcium carbonate deposition.&lt;SEP&gt;An ion ($\mathrm{CO}_3^{2-}$) present in soil solution that influences calcium carbonate deposition and dissolution.&lt;SEP&gt;The carbonate ion is an anion that competes with other ligands for binding with cadmium ions in aqueous solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbonate ion is a negatively charged chemical species that reacts with rare earth elements to form a precipitate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791119375</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bicarbonate Ion">
  <data key="d0">Bicarbonate Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Bicarbonate Ion (HCO3-) is a macro-ion present in the soil solution that forms associates and participates in calcium carbonate deposition.&lt;SEP&gt;The Bicarbonate Ion is a chemical species whose concentration is tracked as a key parameter in the experimental data.&lt;SEP&gt;The Bicarbonate Ion (HCO3-) is a macro-ion present in the soil solution that forms associates and participates in calcium carbonate deposition.&lt;SEP&gt;The bicarbonate ion is an anion that forms a complex with cadmium ions, influencing adsorption calculations.&lt;SEP&gt;Bicarbonate Ion is a negatively charged anion produced during the hydrolysis of calcium carbonate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791120685</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chloride Ion">
  <data key="d0">Chloride Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Chloride Ion (Cl-) is a macro-ion present in the soil solution that forms associates.&lt;SEP&gt;Chloride Ion (Cl⁻) is a chemical species whose concentration was measured across the experimental conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;The Chloride Ion (Cl-) is a macro-ion present in the soil solution that forms associates.&lt;SEP&gt;The chloride ion is an anion that competes for cadmium binding in the aqueous environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Chloride ions released from NaCl accumulation on the surface help attract calcium ions to form nucleation sites.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydroxide Ion">
  <data key="d0">Hydroxide Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Hydroxide Ion (OH-) is a macro-ion present in the soil solution that forms associates.&lt;SEP&gt;The Hydroxide Ion (OH-) is a macro-ion present in the soil solution that forms associates.&lt;SEP&gt;The hydroxide ion is an anion that forms a complex with cadmium ions, affecting stability constants.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxide Ion is a negatively charged chemical species that reacts with pyrophosphate during hydrolysis.&lt;SEP&gt;An ion that breaks the pyrophosphate bond during the hydrolysis process.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxide Ion is a negatively charged anion released during calcium carbonate dissolution, indicating alkalinity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-026</data>
