<node id="Calcium">
  <data key="d0">Calcium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium is a chemical element and alkaline earth metal that commonly exists as a double-charged cation. It serves as a dominant and major elemental component in phosphogypsum (PG), typically constituting between 20.99% and 24.46% of dried PG samples by weight, though measured mass fractions across various analytical studies range from 2.600% to 28.980%. Alongside sulfur and phosphorus, calcium forms the primary gypsum matrix and is frequently represented in molecular diagrams of potentially toxic elements (PTEs). Its presence in analyzed materials is consistently confirmed through distribution across particle surfaces, a distinct spectral peak near 3.7 keV, and concentration data routinely documented in analytical datasets and tables. Analytically, its molar fraction is calculated based on effective ionic strength, while its concentration is quantified using a volumetric method with EGTA. During acid leaching, calcium dissolves from the gypsum matrix, with concentration peaks indicating dissolution and subsequent recrystallization; experimental tracking shows stable, high initial concentrations that experience a delayed decline when higher doses are introduced. The percentage content is often derived via mathematical formulas in PG analysis, and the element can be recovered using a preparative vertical-flow electrophoresis system or converted into high-value calcium carbonate and calcium oxide.

In geological contexts, calcium acts as the primary cation within the apatite crystal structure, where it is frequently substituted by various trace elements, uranium, and thorium due to comparable ionic radii. It is also replaced by radium, and because calcium shares similar chemical and biological properties with radium, it significantly influences radium’s environmental behavior. From an environmental and agricultural perspective, calcium functions as a chemically activated element and vital plant nutrient when supplied via phosphogypsum to support soil nutrition. It adsorbs to the soil adsorption complex, reinforcing soil aggregate shells, and acts as a cation exchange agent that displaces adsorbed sodium in sodic soils and hydrogen or aluminum in acidic soils, thereby promoting soil flocculation. However, calcium also interacts directly with phosphate in the soil matrix, precipitating it into insoluble forms that reduce phosphate bioavailability to plants. When abundant, it forms complexes with phosphate, effectively reducing competition with sulfate for adsorption sites. Collectively, these chemical, analytical, and ecological characteristics define the measurable behavior and environmental impact of calcium in both laboratory and field settings.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-02