<node id="Quartz">
  <data key="d0">Quartz</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Quartz is a silicate mineral compound and siliceous particle that functions as a typical gangue mineral within phosphate ores. It is also cited in the source text as a standard example mineral. Geologically, it is identified as a siliceous particle present in higher concentrations within the Gabes sediment.

In the context of phosphate processing, quartz operates as a persistent impurity within phosphogypsum, often occurring alongside aluminosilicates. Because of its chemical stability, it remains an insoluble and largely unattacked component that resists dissolution during chemical processing steps. Consequently, it is retained in the residual Beneficiated Solid and is typically discarded during certain beneficiation operations. Additionally, its removal is actively managed to prevent erosion-corrosion damage to industrial reactors.&lt;SEP&gt;Quartz is a common mineral component found in phosphorites, constituting approximately 10% of their composition.&lt;SEP&gt;Quartz is a common impurity mineral found in phosphogypsum, often attributed to silica sources.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
