<node id="Phosphoric acid">
  <data key="d0">Phosphoric acid</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphoric acid is a weaker acid generated as a secondary product during the chemical conversion.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphoric acid is produced from rock phosphate and contributes to organic matter in phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphoric acid is a chemical impurity found in phosphogypsum, existing in various soluble and insoluble forms.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphoric acid is a chemical compound whose production generates phosphogypsum as a major industrial byproduct.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphoric acid is a chemical compound produced by the phosphate fertilizer industry using phosphate rock and sulfuric acid.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791188835</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphate rocks">
  <data key="d0">Phosphate rocks</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphate rocks serve as the raw material feedstock and naturally contain trace amounts of thorium.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate rocks are natural ore deposits containing rare earth elements, estimated at 60-100 billion tons globally.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-000</data>
