<node id="Phosphoric acid manufacturing process">
  <data key="d0">Phosphoric acid manufacturing process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphoric acid manufacturing process is the industrial procedure that converts phosphate rocks into phosphoric acid and generates phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-029</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790942862</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sulfur">
  <data key="d0">Sulfur</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sulfur is a chemical element characterized by its consistent atomic balance across both sides of chemical reaction equations. It serves as a key constituent in various industrial and geological materials, notably functioning as a main component of gypsum and a major elemental component of dried phosphogypsum (PG).

Within phosphogypsum samples, Sulfur is widely distributed across particles and frequently exhibits the highest mass fraction among all major elements. Its presence is spectroscopically identifiable by a small peak around 2.3 keV, and it consistently contributes to the accurate identification of the analyzed material. The concentration of Sulfur in phosphogypsum varies significantly across different samples, with measured mass fractions ranging from 0.006% to 44.450%. Specific analyses have recorded weight percentages of 15.00% and 17.04% in various dried PG samples.

Beyond its prevalence in solid mineral byproducts, Sulfur is also present in syngas mixtures, where it can be effectively trapped by oxygen carriers during processing. Its detection in analyzed materials remains a critical factor for material characterization and identification across these diverse chemical and industrial contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfur is a plant nutrient supplied by phosphogypsum to agricultural soils to support plant nutrition.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-028</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189074</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Oxygen">
  <data key="d0">Oxygen</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Oxygen is a fundamental chemical element that serves critical functions across various chemical, mineralogical, and industrial applications. In its standard molecular state, it exists as a diatomic molecule, frequently represented by two red spheres, which actively drives oxidation reactions. As a staple of chemical stoichiometry, the atom count of oxygen is strictly balanced on both sides of reaction equations to ensure mass conservation. Structurally, oxygen is a primary constituent of quartz, where it undergoes substitution by fluorine during the rate-determining step of specific mineralogical reactions.

In the context of phosphogypsum, oxygen is the most abundant element, consistently comprising between 52.01% and 58.02% of the dried material by weight. This dominance is clearly evidenced by the tallest peak in energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy spectra, making oxygen a definitive marker for identifying phosphogypsum in analyzed samples. Beyond its role as a structural and analytical identifier, oxygen within phosphogypsum can be strategically utilized as an oxygen carrier in chemical looping gasification processes. Additionally, oxygen is explicitly depicted in the molecular diagrams of PTEs, further highlighting its widespread presence in both natural geological formations and engineered chemical systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791090871</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
