<node id="Phosphates">
  <data key="d0">Phosphates</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphates are a class of chemical compounds that manifest in two distinct contexts: industrial byproducts and natural soil minerals. In industrial settings, particularly within phosphogypsum, phosphates function as primary potentially toxic impurities. Their concentration and variation are heavily dependent on the specific wet process used during production. During phosphogypsum purification, phosphates are systematically removed, yet they frequently adhere to gypsum crystals and can substitute directly into the crystal lattice. In leaching operations, they dominate the undissolved particles found in the residue. Their chemical behavior is complex; their dissolution can be significantly hindered by surrounding gypsum coatings, and their solubility has been explicitly determined in scientific studies. Furthermore, under certain conditions, these phosphogypsum-bound phosphates undergo microbial reduction, generating phosphine gas.

In a separate natural context, phosphates also refer to specific green minerals embedded within soil profiles. These naturally occurring phosphate minerals are notably composed of magnesium and potassium. This dual characterization highlights the entity's varied presence across both industrial waste management and natural geological environments, where it manifests either as a chemically reactive impurity or as a stable, metal-rich mineral component.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphates are impurities present in phosphogypsum solids.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fluorides">
  <data key="d0">Fluorides</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Fluorides are a class of chemical compounds, predominantly existing as sodium and calcium compounds, that serve as significant impurities within phosphogypsum, a byproduct generated during phosphate rock processing. Their concentration in phosphogypsum is intrinsically linked to the source rock and is subject to anion substitution mechanisms. A defining characteristic of these compounds is their solubility, which facilitates their migration into surrounding soil and groundwater aquifers. Because fluorides are classified as primary potentially toxic elements, their leaching potential poses substantial environmental contamination and public health risks. To mitigate these hazards and ensure compliance with regulatory frameworks, fluorides must be systematically extracted during the purification process to meet stringent safety and quality standards.

In addition to environmental and health concerns, elevated fluoride levels critically undermine the engineering viability of phosphogypsum when it is repurposed as construction backfill. High concentrations of these compounds actively degrade pore structure formation and inhibit hydration product precipitation within cemented paste backfill (CPB) matrices. As fluoride content increases, it directly correlates with a marked decline in the material's workability and mechanical performance, resulting in significant strength reductions. Consequently, precise control and removal of fluorides are essential not only for environmental protection but also for preserving the structural integrity and functional reliability of phosphogypsum-based construction applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluorides are dispersed soluble Rare Earth Element salts whose dissolution may be hindered by gypsum coating.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluorides are chemical compounds present in phosphogypsum alongside phosphates.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluorides are impurities present in phosphogypsum solids.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
