<node id="Table 2">
  <data key="d0">Table 2</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Table 2 refers to a generic data table label used across multiple scientific studies, with its specific content varying significantly depending on the research context. When synthesizing the provided descriptions, it is evident that multiple distinct instances of Table 2 exist, each serving a different analytical purpose. These instances can be categorized into four primary scientific domains: phosphogypsum chemical characterization, phosphogypsum impurity classification, radionuclide activity assessment, and composite bone cement phase analysis.

In the context of phosphogypsum characterization, one version of Table 2 presents comprehensive chemical analysis results. This includes the chemical composition percentages of DA and HDS phosphogypsum samples, as well as the elemental compositions of dried phosphogypsum determined via X-ray fluorescence analysis. Additionally, this table details the concentrations of rare earth elements and major metal elements in specific samples labeled PG1 and PG2, utilizing inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry and inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry to report both rare earth element and mineral data.

Another distinct iteration of Table 2 focuses on the classification and radiological properties of phosphogypsum. One dataset categorizes various impurities found in phosphogypsum by their type, primary chemical form, and solubility characteristics. A separate but related instance presents the activity concentration statistics of different radionuclides, providing quantitative data for both phosphogypsum and phosphate rock samples to assess radiological profiles.

Finally, a fourth distinct context applies Table 2 to materials science, specifically in the formulation of composite bone cements. In this application, the table presents the quantitative phase composition percentages of various components within the cement matrix, detailing the proportional distribution of different material phases used in the composite structure.&lt;SEP&gt;A reference table detailing the types and primary forms of impurities found in phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Table 2 documents the effects of phosphogypsum biofertilizer application on the growth and yield of ryegrass and pakchoi.&lt;SEP&gt;Data table referenced in the text regarding the positive effects of phosphogypsum biofertilizers on plant growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Table 2 illustrates the minor element composition of phosphogypsum depending on the phosphate rock source.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005</data>
