<node id="Fluoride Ion">
  <data key="d0">Fluoride Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Fluoride Ion is a negatively charged chemical species that reacts with calcium to form precipitates.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluoride ion is a chemical component often found in phosphogypsum waste, requiring immobilization to prevent environmental contamination.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791022627</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Precipitation Reaction">
  <data key="d0">Precipitation Reaction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Precipitation Reaction describes the chemical process where soluble ions combine to form an insoluble solid.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790961592</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrolysis Reaction">
  <data key="d0">Hydrolysis Reaction</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydrolysis Reaction describes the chemical breakdown of polyphosphates through reaction with hydroxide ions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790961592</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphate Fertilizer Production">
  <data key="d0">Phosphate Fertilizer Production</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphate Fertilizer Production is a specialized industrial process dedicated to the manufacturing of agricultural fertilizers derived from phosphate rock. Central to this operation is the chemical treatment of phosphate minerals, typically through acidulation, which facilitates the extraction of phosphoric acid for fertilizer formulation. This core manufacturing activity is fundamentally characterized by the concurrent generation of phosphogypsum (PG) as a substantial chemical byproduct.

Throughout the production lifecycle, phosphogypsum emerges as a primary residue and a major solid waste material. The consistent formation of this gypsum-rich compound is an inherent outcome of the chemical reactions involved in processing phosphate ores. Consequently, the management and disposal of phosphogypsum represent a critical operational and environmental consideration, directly tied to the scale and methodology of phosphate fertilizer manufacturing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791209442</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Polyphosphates">
  <data key="d0">Polyphosphates</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Polyphosphates are chemical compounds found in phosphogypsum that undergo hydrolysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790961592</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gypsum Slurry">
  <data key="d0">Gypsum Slurry</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
