<node id="Radioactive Elements">
  <data key="d0">Radioactive Elements</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Radioactive elements are hazardous components found in phosphogypsum that require monitoring and removal.&lt;SEP&gt;Radioactive elements are naturally occurring substances found in elevated concentrations within phosphogypsum and its surrounding environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Hazardous substances found in phosphogypsum that necessitate environmental monitoring.&lt;SEP&gt;Radioactive elements are hazardous substances present in phosphogypsum that can be absorbed into the human body through inhalation, oral, or skin contact.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791109093</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uranium-238">
  <data key="d0">Uranium-238</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Uranium-238 is a radioactive isotope and the foundational parent of the U-238 decay series, naturally prevalent in sedimentary phosphates and derived from phosphate rocks. It is a notable component of phosphogypsum waste, where it is routinely analyzed for its activity concentration and utilized to calculate isotopic ratios alongside Uranium-234 and Uranium-235. Within this industrial byproduct, Uranium-238 typically functions as a minor radionuclide, with activity ratios relative to Radium-226 and Lead-210 generally remaining below unity. However, under specific environmental or measurement conditions, its activity can equal that of Radium-226. Chemically, the isotope exhibits high mobility in the U(VI) oxidation state.

The radioactive decay of Uranium-238 generates Lead-210, a contaminant frequently detected in phosphogypsum leachate. Due to its presence in these waste materials and surrounding environmental matrices, Uranium-238 poses distinct ecological and health considerations. It can accumulate in human tissues through various exposure pathways, underscoring the importance of monitoring its concentration, mobility, and decay products in phosphogypsum-related settings.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-023</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189431</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Radium-226">
  <data key="d0">Radium-226</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Radium-226 is a naturally occurring radioactive isotope and the most abundant form of radium, serving as a key member of the uranium-238 decay series. It is typically found in secular equilibrium with uranium-238 and is generated through the alpha decay of thorium-230. Primarily sourced from sedimentary phosphate rocks and uranium mill waste solutions, Radium-226 is notably concentrated in Florida phosphate materials and other geological formations.

In industrial and environmental contexts, Radium-226 is the principal source of radioactivity in phosphogypsum waste, stacks, and yards, where its activity levels frequently match or exceed those of uranium-238 and are significantly higher than in fly ash or natural soil. Due to its chemical similarity to calcium, it readily integrates into environmental and biological systems. The isotope persists in soils even after ecological restoration, migrates through groundwater systems, and is actively monitored in drinking water to ensure radiological safety.

As a gamma-emitting radionuclide, Radium-226 is quantified through gamma emission measurements and serves as the direct parent isotope for radon-222. Its specific activity concentration is a fundamental parameter in radiation safety assessments, directly contributing to total absorbed dose rates and gamma radiation exposure. Consequently, it is extensively evaluated for dose conversion factors and environmental transport parameters. Monitoring of Radium-226 concentrations in soil, crops following phosphogypsum application, and aquatic systems is critical for assessing radiation exposure risks. Despite its well-documented environmental impact, speciation modeling of Radium-226 in uranium mill waste solutions has historically encountered inconsistencies due to incomplete thermodynamic data.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-031</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791184499</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lead-210">
  <data key="d0">Lead-210</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Lead-210 (Pb-210) is a radioactive isotope belonging to the uranium-238 decay series. Primarily derived from phosphate rocks, it is a major component of phosphogypsum (PG) waste, where it frequently exhibits enhanced activity concentrations compared to the natural radioactive equilibrium found in the original rock. As a key radionuclide of environmental interest, Lead-210 is routinely measured alongside uranium-238 to assess activity levels in PG waste samples and leachates. Geographic variations exist, with phosphogypsum leachate from Alberta demonstrating significantly higher concentrations than those from Ontario. Chemically, Lead-210 can exist in both +2 and +4 oxidation states and undergoes radioactive decay to form polonium-210.

Due to its environmental mobility and radiological significance, Lead-210 is actively monitored across various environmental media. It is routinely analyzed in air samples collected near phosphogypsum ponds and has been documented to accumulate at significantly elevated levels in plants cultivated on reclaimed phosphate mine lands. To address potential health and ecological risks, the isotope is extensively evaluated for dose conversion factors and environmental transport parameters. These assessments support comprehensive radiological safety protocols and inform the management of uranium-series radionuclides in industrial and reclaimed environments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-031</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791186032</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Polonium-210">
  <data key="d0">Polonium-210</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Polonium-210 is a radioactive element derived from phosphate rocks and present in phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Polonium-210 is a radionuclide that partitions into phosphogypsum during chemical processing of phosphate rock.&lt;SEP&gt;Polonium-210 is a radionuclide analyzed in air samples taken near phosphogypsum ponds.&lt;SEP&gt;A radionuclide whose levels in forage and soil were assessed following low-rate phosphogypsum application.&lt;SEP&gt;Polonium-210 is a relatively long-lived radionuclide found in phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;A relatively long-lived radionuclide that partitions into phosphogypsum and is a major alpha emitter in groundwater.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-031</data>
