<node id="Aluminum Oxide">
  <data key="d0">Aluminum Oxide</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A mineral compound found in phosphogypsum with concentrations ranging from 1219.37 to 1312.60 ppm.&lt;SEP&gt;Aluminum Oxide is a main metal element compound measured in WBS and TWBS samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Aluminum Oxide is a compound whose concentration increases in the beneficiated concentrates alongside rare earth elements.&lt;SEP&gt;Aluminum oxide is a chemical compound listed in the compositional analysis table.&lt;SEP&gt;Aluminum oxide is an impurity component found in phosphogypsum alongside other transition metals.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791088448</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sodium Sulfate">
  <data key="d0">Sodium Sulfate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sodium Sulfate is a compound eliminated during the washing process, causing a significant decrease in sodium oxide concentration.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium sulfate is a chemical compound extracted from Moroccan phosphogypsum waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium Sulfate is a chemical compound produced alongside calcium silicate during the transformation of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium sulfate is a chemical additive tested alongside sulfuric acid to mitigate leaching efficiency drops.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium sulfate is a chemical additive tested in the cycled aqueous solution that failed to improve rare earth element leaching efficiency.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791039701</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Beneficiation Process">
  <data key="d0">Beneficiation Process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The beneficiation process is an elemental-independent technique used to enrich rare earth elements and other compounds in concentrates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983741</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scanning Electron Microscope">
  <data key="d0">Scanning Electron Microscope</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The Scanning Electron Microscope is used to analyze the distinct particle morphology and spatial distribution of rare earth elements.&lt;SEP&gt;The scanning electron microscope is an imaging instrument used to visualize the morphology and surface characteristics of phosphogypsum particles.&lt;SEP&gt;A microscopy technique used to capture grayscale micrographs of irregular, chunky particles with rough surfaces.&lt;SEP&gt;An imaging device used to analyze the microstructure of the bone cement samples at 15 kV accelerating voltage.&lt;SEP&gt;Scanning Electron Microscope is a technological instrument used to observe crust structures in soil research.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-037</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791184027</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Energy Dispersive X-Ray Mapping">
  <data key="d0">Energy Dispersive X-Ray Mapping</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Energy Dispersive X-Ray mapping is used alongside scanning electron microscopy to visualize the homogeneous dispersion of rare earth elements on concentrate surfaces.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983741</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="X-Ray Diffraction Analysis">
  <data key="d0">X-Ray Diffraction Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">X-Ray diffraction analysis confirms the elimination of sodium sulfate during the washing process.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983741</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Enrichment Percentage">
  <data key="d0">Enrichment Percentage</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Enrichment Percentage represents the calculated increase in element concentration for the processed samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Enrichment percentage quantifies the increase of rare earth elements and other compounds in beneficiated concentrates relative to the raw phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983741</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Yttrium">
  <data key="d0">Yttrium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Yttrium is a chemical element classified as a rare earth element and is listed after the lanthanides in the referenced table. Recognized as a critical rare earth element by the European Commission, it is a major component found in phosphogypsum and dried phosphogypsum, where it composes over 80 percent of total rare earth elements. It is homogeneously dispersed on the surface of phosphogypsum and beneficiated concentrates.

Scientific and industrial analyses frequently measure the concentration and enrichment of yttrium in various samples, including WBS and TWBS samples. Additionally, the mobility of yttrium from phosphogypsum stacks is evaluated for its environmental and economic implications.&lt;SEP&gt;Yttrium is a trace element enriched in phosphorites, known to substitute for calcium in the apatite crystal structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Yttrium is a chemical element analyzed in phosphogypsum samples with documented concentration data.&lt;SEP&gt;Yttrium is a chemical element that shares similar chemistry with rare earth elements and is often discussed alongside them.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791186384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lanthanum">
  <data key="d0">Lanthanum</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Lanthanum is a chemical element classified as a rare earth element that begins the sequence of increasing atomic number in the referenced data. Analytical detection of the element is characterized by a small spectral peak appearing just after Calcium at approximately 4.0 keV. Researchers have actively measured its concentration and enrichment across various test specimens, including WBS and TWBS samples.

The element is primarily associated with phosphate-derived industrial materials, where it naturally occurs as an impurity in phosphate rock. Lanthanum is highly prevalent in phosphogypsum, notably dominating the elemental composition of dried phosphogypsum. It functions as a major rare earth element that is homogeneously dispersed across the surfaces of both phosphogypsum and beneficiated concentrates, underscoring its significant presence and distribution within these processed materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Lanthanum is a rare earth element enriched in phosphorites relative to shale.&lt;SEP&gt;Lanthanum is a rare earth element analyzed in Idaho phosphogypsum with a mean concentration of 81 mg/kg.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791186384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cerium">
  <data key="d0">Cerium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Cerium (Ce) is a chemical element classified as a specific and excessive Rare Earth Element (RRE) according to Seredin's categorization. It naturally occurs as an impurity in phosphate rock and is highly prevalent in phosphogypsum (PG) and beneficiated concentrates. In dried phosphogypsum, Cerium dominates the overall elemental composition and is homogeneously dispersed across the surface of the material and its concentrates. Its spatial distribution map notably overlaps with the outlined areas of RRE mineral inclusions, and it manifests as distinct clusters that signal the presence of Rare Earth Elements. Analytically, Cerium is identified by characteristic small peaks in the Energy Dispersive X-ray Spectroscopy (EDS) spectrum at approximately 1.0 keV and 4.8 keV.

Throughout various mineral and waste processing stages, the concentration and enrichment of Cerium are systematically measured across diverse sample types, including WBS and TWBS samples. Furthermore, it is actively tracked during leaching processes to monitor real-time concentration variations. These analytical and geochemical characteristics highlight Cerium's significant presence and behavior in phosphate-derived materials, making it a key indicator and target for elemental analysis and recovery operations.&lt;SEP&gt;Cerium is a metal element screened and loaded onto PG to form a compound oxygen carrier.&lt;SEP&gt;Cerium is a rare earth element that does not exhibit enrichment in phosphorites relative to shale.&lt;SEP&gt;Cerium is a rare earth element analyzed in Idaho phosphogypsum with a mean concentration of 36 mg/kg.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791187483</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Neodymium">
  <data key="d0">Neodymium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Neodymium is a chemical element classified as a critical rare earth element, a designation formally recognized by the European Commission and Seredin’s categorization. It is notably prevalent in industrial byproduct streams, particularly phosphogypsum, where it dominates the elemental composition of dried samples. The element is homogeneously dispersed across the surface of phosphogypsum and beneficiated concentrates, and it is also routinely quantified in WBS and TWBS samples, with scientific studies actively measuring its concentration and enrichment levels across these matrices.

In analytical and spectroscopic contexts, Neodymium is reliably identified through distinct spectral signatures. It produces two characteristic peaks at approximately 5.2 keV and 6.5 keV, with the peak around 6.5 keV serving as a primary marker in spectral analysis. These detection methods, combined with its widespread presence in specific geological and industrial materials, highlight Neodymium’s importance as a critical resource and its consistent measurability in environmental and processing samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Neodymium is a rare earth element enriched in the Phosphoria Formation relative to shale.&lt;SEP&gt;Neodymium is a rare earth element analyzed in Idaho phosphogypsum with a mean concentration of 36 mg/kg.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791187056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concentration Factor">
  <data key="d0">Concentration Factor</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Concentration factor represents the total enrichment ratio of rare earth elements from the feed phosphogypsum to the final concentrate.&lt;SEP&gt;Metric indicating a value of 18.42 corresponding to the treatment results.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983741</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="BS">
  <data key="d0">BS</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">BS is the initial material processed before washing and subsequent treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;BS is a processed sample derived from PG, analyzed for elemental concentrations.&lt;SEP&gt;A processed material sample where calcium sulfate dihydrate (CSD) is absent due to high solubility in alkaline EDTA.&lt;SEP&gt;BS is a beneficiated concentrate produced from phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;BS is a beneficiated concentrate material mentioned alongside PG, WBS, CWS, and TWBS as a surface for REE dispersion.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983741</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CWBS">
  <data key="d0">CWBS</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">CWBS is a processed sample derived from PG, analyzed for elemental concentrations.&lt;SEP&gt;CWBS is a matrix containing CaF2 that undergoes crystallization at 700 °C.&lt;SEP&gt;CWBS is a washed and calcined beneficiated concentrate with further increased rare earth element concentration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012</data>
