<node id="CWS">
  <data key="d0">CWS</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">CWS is a beneficiated concentrate material with a chemical composition similar to WBS but with slightly increased element concentrations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calcium Oxide (CaO)">
  <data key="d0">Calcium Oxide (CaO)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium oxide is a major chemical component in PG, WBS, and TWBS, with concentrations varying due to gypsum solubility and processing steps.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium oxide is a chemical compound whose mean weight percentage in PG is analyzed across different countries.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical phase identified by red triangles in the diffraction graph.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium oxide is a product of PSC technology that is subsequently used for Portland cement production.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791207316</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sulfur Trioxide (SO3)">
  <data key="d0">Sulfur Trioxide (SO3)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sulfur trioxide is a major chemical component in PG that decreases in WBS due to the high solubility of gypsum in EDTA.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfur trioxide is a chemical compound whose mean weight percentage in PG is analyzed across different countries.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791023386</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Silicon Dioxide (SiO2)">
  <data key="d0">Silicon Dioxide (SiO2)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Silicon dioxide is a major component in WBS and TWBS, primarily originating from quartz which resists dissolution during processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Silicon dioxide is a chemical compound whose mean weight percentage in PG is analyzed across different countries.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical phase identified by black diamonds in the diffraction graph.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791090619</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fluorine (F)">
  <data key="d0">Fluorine (F)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Fluorine is a chemical component that increases in WBS and TWBS due to the formation of stable metal fluorides during processing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sodium Fluorosilicates">
  <data key="d0">Sodium Fluorosilicates</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Crystallized compounds identified in TWBS, formed from the transformation of solubilized quartz.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium fluorosilicates are compounds identified in TWBS following hydrofluoric acid treatment of WBS.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphorus Pentoxide (P2O5)">
  <data key="d0">Phosphorus Pentoxide (P2O5)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphorus pentoxide is a potential REE-bearing compound whose concentration positively correlates with REE enrichment in WBS and CWBS.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Metal Fluoride">
  <data key="d0">Metal Fluoride</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Metal fluoride is a stable compound formed during PG dissolution, contributing to increased fluorine concentrations in residual solids.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Metal Phosphate">
  <data key="d0">Metal Phosphate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Metal phosphate is a compound formed during the first enrichment process using EDTA, which may transform into metal fluorides in later steps.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gangue">
  <data key="d0">Gangue</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Gangue refers to undesirable mineral phases like SiO2 that are efficiently solubilized during processing to enrich REEs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rare Earth Phosphate">
  <data key="d0">Rare Earth Phosphate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Rare earth phosphate is a compound that may transform into rare earth fluoride during hydrofluoric acid treatment, though this process remains uncertain.&lt;SEP&gt;Rare earth phosphate is a dispersed compound identified in the enriched rare earth concentrates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rare Earth Fluoride">
  <data key="d0">Rare Earth Fluoride</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Rare earth fluoride is a potential product of rare earth phosphate transformation during hydrofluoric acid treatment of WBS.&lt;SEP&gt;Rare earth fluoride is a dispersed compound identified in the enriched rare earth concentrates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Independent Rare Earth Minerals">
  <data key="d0">Independent Rare Earth Minerals</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Independent rare earth minerals represent one of the natural occurrence modes of REEs alongside weathered crust deposits and isomorphism substitution.&lt;SEP&gt;A natural occurrence mode of REEs in nature.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Weathered Crust Elution Deposits">
  <data key="d0">Weathered Crust Elution Deposits</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Weathered crust elution deposits represent a natural occurrence mode of REEs influenced by ionic radii and hydration energy.&lt;SEP&gt;A natural occurrence mode of REEs in nature.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ionic Radii">
  <data key="d0">Ionic Radii</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Ionic radii are a key factor determining the distribution and speciation of REEs in PG and other matrices.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ions Interactions">
  <data key="d0">Ions Interactions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Ions interactions influence the spatial distribution and chemical behavior of REEs within the PG crystalline structure.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="REEs Ion Hydration Energy">
  <data key="d0">REEs Ion Hydration Energy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">REEs ion hydration energy affects the distribution and speciation of rare earth elements in phosphogypsum matrices.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PG Crystalline Structure">
  <data key="d0">PG Crystalline Structure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The crystalline structure of PG dictates how REEs are distributed and bound within the material.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
