<node id="U-235">
  <data key="d0">U-235</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">U-235 is a naturally occurring, radioactive uranium isotope and radionuclide characterized by a shorter half-life than U-238, which contributes to its lower natural abundance of approximately 0.72% in the environment. It functions as a parent isotope, and its relative scarcity results in the lower prevalence of the thorium isotopes within its decay series. Additionally, the natural isotopic ratio between U-235 and U-238 remains stable at approximately 21.7 across analyzed samples.

In environmental and industrial monitoring, U-235 is frequently tracked and analyzed using specific radiometric techniques. Its activity concentration has been determined in various studied samples, including phosphogypsum waste and process water at multiple industrial facilities, as well as in groundwater beneath the AMAX site. Specific field measurements have also been documented in Florida, where a recorded value of 6 was noted. Analytical protocols for U-235 involve alpha-measurements for waste analysis, while its minimum detectable activity is calculated using Currie's formula.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791185119</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="U-234">
  <data key="d0">U-234</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Uranium-234 (U-234) is a naturally occurring, radioactive isotope of uranium with an average environmental abundance of 0.0056%. As the decay daughter of Thorium-234, it generally maintains expected equilibrium with U-238 during radionuclide fractionation. However, in specific hydrological environments like groundwater, the isotope demonstrates notable enrichment relative to U-238 due to the alpha recoil ejection of Th-234.

Analytically, U-234 is routinely quantified alongside U-235 and U-238 using alpha-particle spectrometry and alpha-measurements, with its minimum detectable activity calculated via Currie's formula. In environmental and industrial monitoring, the isotope has been extensively analyzed in phosphogypsum waste samples and process water across multiple sites, where it is frequently reported to exhibit high activity concentrations. Field assessments have also documented its presence in regions such as Florida, with specific measurements recording a value of 130.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791185119</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="U-232">
  <data key="d0">U-232</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">U-232 is a radioactive isotope supplied by Amersham used to spike samples during chemical preparation.&lt;SEP&gt;U-232 is a tracer isotope added during the radiochemical procedure to assess recovery rates.&lt;SEP&gt;U-232 is a natural uranium isotope analyzed in phosphogypsum waste samples using alpha-measurements.</data>
