</node>
<node id="Thorium-232">
  <data key="d0">Thorium-232</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Thorium-232 is a naturally occurring, radioactive isotope and radionuclide that belongs to the Th-232 decay series. As a gamma-emitting isotope, its activity concentration is typically determined by measuring the gamma emissions of its decay daughters, specifically Lead-212 (Pb-212) and Actinium-228 (Ac-228). For radiological assessment purposes, the gamma radiation dose rate emitted by Thorium-232 is often converted into an equivalent Radium value.

Geologically, Thorium-232 is typically found in appreciable amounts within igneous phosphates. It also occurs as a minor radionuclide in various environmental and industrial matrices. In Saskatchewan soils, it has been measured with a specific activity of 24 Bq kg⁻¹. Furthermore, Thorium-232 is a notable component of phosphogypsum waste, where it exists alongside other radioactive elements. In these waste samples, its activity concentration is routinely measured alongside other isotopes, and it contributes to the total absorbed dose rate associated with the material.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791184499</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Potassium-40">
  <data key="d0">Potassium-40</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Radionuclide measured at 1461 keV to determine potassium content in samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium-40 is a radioactive isotope whose activity concentration is measured in PG waste samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium-40 is a naturally occurring gamma-emitting isotope that contributes to the total absorbed dose rate in phosphogypsum waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium-40 is a minor radionuclide found in phosphogypsum waste with lower activity compared to Radium-226 and Lead-210.&lt;SEP&gt;A natural radioactive isotope whose gamma radiation dose rate is converted to an equivalent Radium value.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Radon-222">
  <data key="d0">Radon-222</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Radon-222 is a naturally occurring radioactive gas and a decay product of Radium-226. It is predominantly associated with phosphogypsum (PG) waste and stacks, where it is released and exhaled from the surface into the surrounding atmosphere. Referenced in scientific literature with varying longevity characteristics, the isotope is evaluated using the radon emanation fraction index, with measured emanation fractions typically ranging from 18% to 25%. In environmental monitoring, Radon-222 serves as a critical tracer for characterizing the redistribution of Technologically Enhanced Naturally Occurring Radioactive Material (TE-NORM).

Research extensively analyzes the exhalation rates and atmospheric concentrations of Radon-222 near phosphogypsum stacks, frequently comparing these measurements to natural background levels. Emission rates from these stacks vary significantly, and the gas poses documented health risks when inhaled by humans. Due to its environmental presence and public health implications, the atmospheric release of Radon-222 from gypsum stacks is regulated by the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (E.P.A.).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-045</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791185905</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Radon-220">
  <data key="d0">Radon-220</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Radon-220 is a short-lived radon gas isotope compared to Radon-222 in terms of half-life.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Radon-219">
  <data key="d0">Radon-219</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Radon-219 is a short-lived radon gas isotope compared to Radon-222 in terms of half-life.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Radium Equivalent Activity">
  <data key="d0">Radium Equivalent Activity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Radium Equivalent Activity is a radiological index that combines the hazards of Ra-226, Th-232, and K-40 into a single quantity.&lt;SEP&gt;Radium equivalent activity is a radiation hazard index metric calculated to assess radiological risk in waste samples.&lt;SEP&gt;A weighted sum of natural radionuclide activities used to assess radiation hazards in building materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Absorbed Gamma Dose Rate">
  <data key="d0">Absorbed Gamma Dose Rate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Absorbed gamma dose rate is a radiological index measuring the total absorbed dose from gamma emissions, found to be approximately four times higher than international safety limits in phosphogypsum waste.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Radon Emanation Fraction">
  <data key="d0">Radon Emanation Fraction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Radiation hazard index used to evaluate radon release from phosphogypsum wastes.&lt;SEP&gt;Radon Emanation Fraction is a calculated parameter representing the proportion of Radon-222 gas released from PG waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Radon emanation fraction is a radiological index used to evaluate the amount of radon gas released from the samples.&lt;SEP&gt;The radon emanation fraction represents the percentage of radon released from phosphogypsum waste, averaging 22%.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Radiation Hazard Indices">
  <data key="d0">Radiation Hazard Indices</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A set of metrics used to evaluate the environmental and health risks posed by phosphogypsum wastes.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation hazard indices are a set of metrics used to evaluate the radiological risk associated with phosphogypsum waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation Hazard Indices are metrics calculated using formulas like Ra-Eq for radiological safety assessments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Coefficient of Variance">
  <data key="d0">Coefficient of Variance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Coefficient of variance is a statistical measure indicating the relative variability of the radiation hazard index measurements.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Combined Uncertainty">
  <data key="d0">Combined Uncertainty</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Combined uncertainty is a statistical parameter representing the measurement uncertainty for the average values of the radiation indices.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equation 2">
  <data key="d0">Equation 2</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation 2 is a mathematical formula used to calculate the total absorbed dose rate values from gamma emissions in phosphogypsum waste samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equation 3">
  <data key="d0">Equation 3</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation 3 is a mathematical formula used to calculate the radon emanation fraction to quantify radon gas release.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Half-life">
  <data key="d0">Half-life</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Half-life is the time required for a radioactive isotope to decay to half its initial amount, a key property for evaluating radon gas behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;A temporal property of radioactive isotopes determining decay rates and ingrowth of products.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189670</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="55 nGy/h">
  <data key="d0">55 nGy/h</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">55 nGy/h is the international recommended safe dose level for workers exposed to gamma radiation, established by UNSCEAR.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Radium Equivalent Index">
  <data key="d0">Radium Equivalent Index</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A radiological safety metric used to evaluate actual radioactivity in materials containing naturally occurring radionuclides.&lt;SEP&gt;The radium equivalent index is a radiation hazard index calculated for phosphogypsum waste, found to exceed 370 Bq/kg.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Alpha-Particle Measurements">
  <data key="d0">Alpha-Particle Measurements</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Analytical technique used to calculate natural isotopic ratios of uranium isotopes in the samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
