<node id="CaSO4">
  <data key="d0">CaSO4</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">CaSO4, the chemical formula for calcium sulfate, is a compound with distinct roles in both waste composition and advanced chemical processing. It serves as the primary phase in phosphogypsum (PG) waste, where it constitutes the characteristic gypsum matrix. The compound can be generated through the oxidation of calcium sulfide (CaS) and is also produced as a complex salt during ion exchange reactions.

In industrial and energy applications, CaSO4 functions as an oxygen carrier in chemical looping gasification (CLG) and combustion systems. Within these processes, it actively reacts with gasification products and undergoes subsequent reduction steps. From a computational and physical standpoint, CaSO4 forms the granular structure of the oxygen carrier and is assigned a defined surface energy in kinetic models to accurately simulate its reaction dynamics. Collectively, these properties highlight its versatility across environmental waste management and high-temperature chemical conversion technologies.&lt;SEP&gt;CaSO4 (calcium sulfate) is a precipitate formed during sulfate removal.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189785</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
