<node id="Radon">
  <data key="d0">Radon</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Radon is a radioactive gas that belongs to the uranium decay series and serves as a prominent contributor to natural background radiation. It is primarily generated through the radioactive decay of Radium-226 within soil, from which it naturally emanates into the surrounding environment. Beyond soil, Radon is also released from Naturally Occurring Radioactive Material (NORM)-contaminated substances, various building materials, and notably from phosphogypsum and phosphogypsum stacks.

Due to its radioactive emissions and the hazards posed by its decay progeny, Radon presents significant health, environmental, and residential occupancy risks. Consequently, it is a central subject in radiation hazard and exposure assessments, where scientific studies routinely measure its concentrations and evaluate potential safety thresholds. Research indicates that in certain industrial contexts, such as phosphogypsum stacks, Radon concentrations often align with typical indoor levels and remain well below established remedial action limits, though its pervasive presence continues to warrant careful monitoring and comprehensive risk evaluation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791187781</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
