<node id="Potentially Toxic Elements">
  <data key="d0">Potentially Toxic Elements</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Potentially Toxic Elements are hazardous chemical substances and primary contaminants present within phosphogypsum, whose specific concentration and chemical speciation directly dictate the material's overall toxicity and hazard potential. These elements, which encompass hazardous impurities such as fluorides and phosphates, pose significant environmental and public health risks while raising substantial safety concerns regarding the commercial and agricultural applications of phosphogypsum. Because these contaminants can leach from phosphogypsum stockpiles and subsequently accumulate or transport to surrounding areas, their environmental release and bioavailability represent critical operational and ecological concerns.

Effective management of phosphogypsum therefore requires the careful retention and strict control of Potentially Toxic Elements to prevent uncontrolled environmental transfer. Remediation strategies specifically target these hazardous substances for immobilization, often utilizing cemented paste backfill (CPB) matrices to securely contain them. Ultimately, minimizing the bioavailability and mobility of Potentially Toxic Elements remains a fundamental objective in remediation processes, ensuring that their hazards are mitigated and that phosphogypsum can be managed safely for industrial and environmental purposes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791020974</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
