<node id="Hydrogen Fluoride">
  <data key="d0">Hydrogen Fluoride</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydrogen fluoride (HF) is a gaseous chemical compound that typically manifests in the gas phase when temperatures reach 600 °C, with its concentration increasing as temperatures rise. In molecular representations, it is depicted as a light blue sphere bonded to a grey or white sphere. This compound is primarily generated as a byproduct during phosphate processing operations, specifically through the acidulation of fluorapatite, the reaction of phosphate rock with sulfuric acid, and the high-temperature decomposition of fluorine-containing acids. These industrial processes simultaneously yield phosphoric acid and phosphogypsum, with hydrogen fluoride being released throughout the reaction and processing stages.

Due to its frequent release during phosphate production and from phosphogypsum storage yards, hydrogen fluoride poses significant environmental and health hazards. As a toxic and hazardous gas, its emissions contribute to local air pollution and broader environmental contamination. Furthermore, exposure to this compound is known to cause cellular damage, underscoring its classification as a dangerous industrial byproduct that requires careful management to mitigate ecological and biological risks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189074</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
