<node id="Recrystallization">
  <data key="d0">Recrystallization</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Recrystallization is a process that occurs after the dissolution of the gypsum matrix surface during leaching.&lt;SEP&gt;Recrystallization is a leaching reinforcement method recommended to enhance the release of soluble rare earth elements from the gypsum matrix.&lt;SEP&gt;Recrystallization is a physical-chemical phenomenon where gypsum plates reform, releasing entrained rare earth elements into the solution.&lt;SEP&gt;Recrystallization is a method used to capture carbon dioxide and decompose phosphogypsum slag to obtain high-purity calcium carbonate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791088931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Leaching Kinetics">
  <data key="d0">Leaching Kinetics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Leaching Kinetics refers to the rate and efficiency of Rare Earth Element extraction, which is hindered by the dense structure of undissolved mineral inclusions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791039701</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Table S1">
  <data key="d0">Table S1</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Table S1 provides data indicating that the Rare Earth Element content in leached Phosphogypsum is approximately 13 wt%.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-006</data>
