<node id="Barium">
  <data key="d0">Barium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Barium is an alkaline earth chemical element that frequently occurs as a trace component or patchy impurity within various geological and industrial materials. In phosphorite deposits, it is notably depleted relative to crustal abundances, while in dried phosphogypsum, it constitutes approximately 0.54 wt% and is consistently measured across multiple scientific studies, including those focused on South African samples. Chemically, barium readily co-precipitates and co-crystallizes with radium, including the radioisotope Ra-226, to form sulfate compounds and solid solutions. This interaction is particularly significant in uranium mining environments, where it contributes to the formation of (Ba, Ra) sulfate sludges, and in waste management systems, where it plays a critical role in influencing radium nucleation, solubility, and overall mobility.

Due to its widespread presence in these matrices, barium is routinely analyzed alongside radium and strontium in phosphogypsum leachates and is monitored in conjunction with natural radioisotopes during groundwater assessments. Given its environmental distribution and potential health implications, barium is subject to varying regulatory limits, most notably a World Health Organization guideline value of 0.1 mg l⁻¹ for water quality.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791184027</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strontium">
  <data key="d0">Strontium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Strontium is an alkaline earth chemical element that occurs naturally as a trace component in both phosphorites and phosphogypsum. In phosphorite deposits, it is notably enriched and readily substitutes for calcium within the apatite crystal structure. Geochemically, strontium tends to co-precipitate and co-crystallize with radium (specifically Ra-226) and barium, often forming sulfate compounds and solid solutions. This co-precipitation behavior is particularly significant for understanding radium mobility in industrial waste systems.

Analytical assessments of phosphogypsum have characterized strontium as a measurable trace element, with mass contents typically ranging from 47.00 to 469.71 μg/g. In dried phosphogypsum samples, it constitutes approximately 0.54 wt% of the material and can be spectroscopically identified as a small peak around 1.5 keV. Due to its presence and mobility characteristics, strontium is actively studied in the context of phosphogypsum leachates and its subsequent environmental behavior in soil systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791186664</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
