<node id="Radionuclides">
  <data key="d0">Radionuclides</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Radionuclides are radioactive isotopes and elements that occur naturally or are generated through industrial processes, and they are formally classified as a distinct category of substances within regulatory data tables. These materials are notably present in industrial byproducts such as phosphogypsum, where they pose environmental risks through emission and permeation into water bodies. Additionally, they can be transported by nanoparticles from phosphogypsum sources, and their environmental mobility and release mechanisms have been extensively investigated in scientific literature. The distribution and behavior of radionuclides in soils and effluents are also well-documented across multiple studies.

Once dispersed into the environment, radionuclides can be leached from industrial stacks and accumulate in contaminated soils, where they are readily absorbed by agricultural plants. This uptake facilitates their transfer through the food chain, ultimately exposing humans and animals to potential health risks. The combined pathways of water permeation, soil contamination, and biological accumulation underscore the significant environmental and public health concerns associated with radionuclides, driving continued research into their ecological impact and regulatory management.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-031</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791188093</data>
