  <data key="d2">A researcher whose procedure for slow cooling thermally decomposed samples was followed in the hydroxyapatite synthesis process.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="High-Energy Vibro-Milling Method">
  <data key="d0">High-Energy Vibro-Milling Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mechanical processing technique used to grind thermally decomposed bone samples into hydroxyapatite nano-powder.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bioactivity">
  <data key="d0">Bioactivity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Bioactivity is the ability of the material to interact favorably with biological systems, such as forming bone bonds.&lt;SEP&gt;A key material property evaluated in vitro, which is enhanced by the addition of sodium chloride to the composite bone cement.&lt;SEP&gt;Bioactivity is a desired characteristic of biomaterials evaluated by their ability to form apatite layers in simulated environments.&lt;SEP&gt;The material's inherent capability to promote bone-like apatite formation directly on its surface.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Injectability">
  <data key="d0">Injectability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A critical performance characteristic of the composite bone cement that is expected to remain excellent across different sodium chloride concentrations.&lt;SEP&gt;A functional property of the cement paste indicating its ease of delivery and resistance to disintegration in solution.&lt;SEP&gt;Injectability is a physical property of the composite bone cement evaluated during the research work.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="In Vitro Evaluation">
  <data key="d0">In Vitro Evaluation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Laboratory testing procedures used to assess the apatite-forming ability and cytotoxicity of the composite materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mortar">
  <data key="d0">Mortar</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A laboratory tool used to grind thermally decomposed bone samples into smaller pieces during hydroxyapatite synthesis.&lt;SEP&gt;Mortar is a construction material manufactured using β-building gypsum powder recovered by the PBG technology.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791208777</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Washout Resistance">
  <data key="d0">Washout Resistance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A performance metric evaluated for the composite bone cement to determine its stability in wet environments.&lt;SEP&gt;A performance metric measuring the cement's ability to retain its structural integrity and shape when immersed in fluid.&lt;SEP&gt;Washout resistance is a physical property of the composite bone cement evaluated during the research work.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="In Vitro Apatite-Forming Ability">
  <data key="d0">In Vitro Apatite-Forming Ability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A bioactivity characteristic studied to evaluate the composite's potential for bone integration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="In Vitro Cytotoxicity">
  <data key="d0">In Vitro Cytotoxicity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A biocompatibility metric assessed through cell culture analysis to ensure the material is safe for biomedical use.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="0.70CaS–0.30HAP Composite">
  <data key="d0">0.70CaS–0.30HAP Composite</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The base composite material selected for the study, which was later modified with varying amounts of sodium chloride.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="NaCl Porogens">
  <data key="d0">NaCl Porogens</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sodium chloride particles used in scaffold fabrication to create porous structures and improve interconnectivity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Powder-to-Liquid Ratio">
  <data key="d0">Powder-to-Liquid Ratio</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Powder-to-liquid ratio refers to the 2:1 proportion of mixed powders to deionized water used to prepare the cement paste.&lt;SEP&gt;A formulation parameter set at 2:1 for mixing the composite bone cement powders with deionized water.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scaffold Interconnectivity">
  <data key="d0">Scaffold Interconnectivity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A structural feature of porous scaffolds that was greatly improved by incorporating NaCl porogens with traditional salt leaching methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calcium Sulfide">
  <data key="d0">Calcium Sulfide</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A primary chemical constituent of the composite bone cement matrix.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium Sulfide is an intermediate compound formed during the thermal decomposition of phosphogypsum under reducing or oxidizing atmospheres.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium sulfide is a decomposition product of phosphogypsum formed at lower temperatures under a reducing atmosphere.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium sulfide is a compound obtained from the hydrolysis of phosphogypsum decomposition slag, forming an alkaline solution with free calcium ions.&lt;SEP&gt;A starting material depicted as a crystal lattice structure composed of yellow and green spheres in the oxidation process.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-029</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791088200</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Amorphous Calcium Phosphate">
  <data key="d0">Amorphous Calcium Phosphate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">An intermediate compound formed during the apatite precipitation mechanism that stabilizes into bone-like apatite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SEM Micrographs">
  <data key="d0">SEM Micrographs</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Scanning electron microscopy images used to visualize the surface and fracture morphology of the composite cements.&lt;SEP&gt;Scanning electron microscope images documenting the surface morphology and crystal growth of the bone cement samples over time.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bone Cement Synthesis Procedure">
  <data key="d0">Bone Cement Synthesis Procedure</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The manufacturing process for creating the composite bone cement, during which NaCl is incorporated.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="In Vitro Apatite-Forming Ability Test">
  <data key="d0">In Vitro Apatite-Forming Ability Test</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An experimental evaluation method used to assess the material's bioactivity and apatite precipitation potential.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Surface Properties">
  <data key="d0">Surface Properties</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Physical and chemical characteristics of the bone cement that are enhanced by NaCl incorporation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ionic Supersaturation">
  <data key="d0">Ionic Supersaturation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A chemical state achieved by adding NaCl, increasing the concentration of Na+ and Cl- ions to drive precipitation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nucleation Sites">
  <data key="d0">Nucleation Sites</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Specific locations on the material surface where ions accumulate to initiate the precipitation of hydroxyapatite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calcium-Rich ACP">
  <data key="d0">Calcium-Rich ACP</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium-rich amorphous calcium phosphate is an intermediate compound formed when negative ions on the hydroxyapatite surface interact with calcium ions in SBF.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calcium-Poor ACP">
  <data key="d0">Calcium-Poor ACP</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium-poor amorphous calcium phosphate is formed when calcium-rich ACP interacts with phosphate ions in SBF, eventually stabilizing into apatite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphate Group">
  <data key="d0">Phosphate Group</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphate groups on the hydroxyapatite surface contribute to negative surface charges and interact with calcium ions to form intermediate compounds.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Biocompatibility">
  <data key="d0">Biocompatibility</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Biocompatibility is a key property exhibited by the x = 2.5 sample, attributed to its porous structure and surface characteristics.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scaffold Grains">
  <data key="d0">Scaffold Grains</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Scaffold grains are the structural components of the bone cement matrix where increased viscosity and stickiness occur between them.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Flake Apatite">
  <data key="d0">Flake Apatite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Flake apatite refers to the tiny, small apatite deposition initially observed on the sample surface after 1 day of SBF immersion.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Needle-Like Apatite">
  <data key="d0">Needle-Like Apatite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Needle-like apatite crystals form and elongate over time, becoming more prominent with higher NaCl content.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cactus-Shaped Apatite">
  <data key="d0">Cactus-Shaped Apatite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Cactus-shaped apatite describes the specific cylindrical and clumped crystal morphology observed on the x = 2.5 sample after 28 days.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydroxyapatite Surface">
  <data key="d0">Hydroxyapatite Surface</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The hydroxyapatite surface reveals negative charges due to hydroxyl and phosphate groups, initiating the apatite precipitation mechanism.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="1-Day Immersion">
  <data key="d0">1-Day Immersion</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The initial 1-day immersion period in SBF results in tiny flake apatite deposition on the composite bone cement surface.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="28-Day Immersion">
  <data key="d0">28-Day Immersion</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The 28-day immersion period in SBF results in fully covered surfaces with cactus-shaped apatite crystals for the x = 2.5 sample.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SmartLab Studio II">
  <data key="d0">SmartLab Studio II</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">SmartLab Studio II is the software program applied to process X-ray diffraction data and calculate quantitative phase composition.&lt;SEP&gt;Specialized software utilized to execute the Reference Intensity Ratio analysis and process X-ray diffraction data.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Syringe">
