<node id="Oxygen Carrier">
  <data key="d0">Oxygen Carrier</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The Oxygen Carrier is a specialized composite material primarily utilized in chemical looping reactions, including chemical looping gasification (CLG) and reduction processes, to facilitate oxygen transfer during fuel conversion. These carriers are typically derived from phosphogypsum, often combined with transition metals, though alternative compositions such as NiO-CaSO4 and CaSO4 are also employed. A defining functional characteristic of the Oxygen Carrier is its ability to supply oxygen while simultaneously lowering overall reaction temperatures.

The operational cycle of the Oxygen Carrier involves distinct procedural phases, including a specific process step frequently indicated by a horizontal arrow that occurs after twenty cycles. To maintain material integrity and efficiency, its grain growth kinetics are actively modeled, and its cyclic performance is stabilized through the incorporation of SiO2. Within the CLG process, the carrier exhibits specific interaction dynamics where CaF2 attaches to the material, while Ca3(PO4)2 particles serve as an adhesive between carrier components. Collectively, these engineered properties enable the Oxygen Carrier to function as a highly stable, cyclically reliable medium for efficient thermochemical oxygen transfer.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-024</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791088448</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
