<node id="Firmicutes">
  <data key="d0">Firmicutes</data>
  <data key="d1">creature</data>
  <data key="d2">Firmicutes is a major and widely distributed bacterial phylum that plays a pivotal ecological role across diverse environments. In composting ecosystems, it serves as a dominant microorganism, comprising approximately 21.95% of the microbial community throughout the entire process. This prevalence is largely attributed to its specialized metabolic functions, particularly its capacity to efficiently decompose plant organic matter and synthesize heat-stable ligninolytic enzymes.

Beyond its role in organic decomposition, the distribution of Firmicutes varies significantly across different sample types, with documented regional variations observed in locations such as the Black Hills. In microbiome research and comparative analyses, Firmicutes is frequently evaluated for relative abundance differences between experimental groups. It is standardly visualized in Circos plots using specific color codes and abundance scales, often illustrated as red ribbons. In these group comparisons, Firmicutes heavily dominates Group A1 samples and maintains a notable connection to Group A2, while also demonstrating a slight abundance preference for the A1_1 subgroup. Collectively, Firmicutes is defined by its metabolic versatility, ecological dominance in decomposition processes, and distinct distribution patterns across both natural and experimental contexts.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-013</data>
