<node id="Absorbed Dose">
  <data key="d0">Absorbed Dose</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Absorbed Dose refers to the average density of energy absorbed by a mass of cells from radiation exposure.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-025</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791183363</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gray">
  <data key="d0">Gray</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Gray is the SI unit for absorbed dose, equivalent to one joule of energy per kilogram of mass.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-025</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791183363</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dose Equivalent">
  <data key="d0">Dose Equivalent</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dose Equivalent measures both the energy absorbed by tissue and its biological impact from radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;A calculated value obtained by multiplying radiation exposure by a weighting factor that accounts for organ radiosensitivity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791183363</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sieverts">
  <data key="d0">Sieverts</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Sieverts are the units used to measure dose equivalent, accounting for the biological impact of different radiation types.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-025</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791183363</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
