<node id="Wet Phosphoric Acid Process">
  <data key="d0">Wet Phosphoric Acid Process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Wet Phosphoric Acid Process is a production method for phosphoric acid that yields phosphogypsum with high impurity levels.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical manufacturing procedure that disrupts radioactive equilibrium and partitions radionuclides into different phases.&lt;SEP&gt;The wet phosphoric acid process is an industrial method that partitions Pb-210 into phosphoric acid.&lt;SEP&gt;The wet phosphoric acid process is the industrial method during which phosphogypsum is produced and treated.&lt;SEP&gt;An industrial process during which Polonium-210 partitions into phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791188249</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Po-210">
  <data key="d0">Po-210</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A radionuclide that partitions primarily into phosphogypsum, with up to 99% appearing in the gypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Po-210 is a radioactive isotope measured in Florida with recorded values of 1030 and 1480.&lt;SEP&gt;Polonium-210 is a relatively long-lived radioactive daughter product formed from the decay of Radon-222.&lt;SEP&gt;A relatively long-lived alpha emitter formed from the decay of Lead-210.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791188410</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dihydrate">
  <data key="d0">Dihydrate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A calcium sulfate form whose recrystallization is slowed by the presence of stabilized hemihydrate sulfate structures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791188559</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Central Florida Rock">
  <data key="d0">Central Florida Rock</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A source of phosphate rock known for producing phosphogypsum with the highest reported Ra-226 activities.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791188559</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Idaho">
  <data key="d0">Idaho</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Idaho is a geographic location representing a source of phosphogypsum samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Idaho is a U.S. state serving as a primary source for phosphogypsum and phosphate rock samples.&lt;SEP&gt;A geographic location associated with phosphate rock that yields phosphogypsum with potentially high radionuclide activities.&lt;SEP&gt;Idaho is a geographic region referenced in the dataset for radioactive isotope measurements.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791188559</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
