<node id="Sample S2">
  <data key="d0">Sample S2</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Sample S2 is a cooling water sample collected from AMAX Phosphate Inc. and USS Agri-Chemicals Plant.&lt;SEP&gt;Sample S2 refers to cooling water and return ditch water collected at AMAX Phosphate Inc. and USS Agri-Chemicals plant.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189431</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sodic Soils">
  <data key="d0">Sodic Soils</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Sodic soils exhibit poor structure due to clay dispersion caused by high sodium levels on the soil exchange complex.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodic soils are alkaline, saline, or saline-sodic soils characterized by high exchangeable sodium levels and a tendency to disperse.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-029</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189431</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gypsum Utilization Coefficient">
  <data key="d0">Gypsum Utilization Coefficient</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The gypsum utilization coefficient is a factor used in rate calculations to account for the efficiency of calcium displacing exchangeable sodium.&lt;SEP&gt;The Gypsum Utilization Coefficient is a parameter used in rate calculations to account for factors affecting calcium displacement efficiency.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-029</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189431</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Exchangeable Sodium Percentage">
  <data key="d0">Exchangeable Sodium Percentage</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Exchangeable Sodium Percentage is a chemical metric that influences the dispersion process and electrolyte requirements.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189431</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Electrolyte Concentration">
  <data key="d0">Electrolyte Concentration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Electrolyte Concentration refers to the ion content in applied water, which determines whether chemical dispersion or physical sealing dominates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189431</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Aggregate Stability">
  <data key="d0">Aggregate Stability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Aggregate Stability is a soil structural property enhanced by calcium flocculation and phosphogypsum application.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189431</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Water Infiltration">
  <data key="d0">Water Infiltration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Water Infiltration is the process of water entering the soil, which is generally enhanced by phosphogypsum-induced changes in soil properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Crusting">
  <data key="d0">Crusting</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Crusting is a surface soil phenomenon that reduces infiltration and increases runoff, which phosphogypsum helps prevent.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Runoff">
  <data key="d0">Runoff</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Runoff is the flow of water over the soil surface, which is decreased by phosphogypsum through enhanced infiltration and aggregate stability.&lt;SEP&gt;A soil erosion factor reduced by phosphogypsum and polyacrylamide applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-031</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Soil Loss">
  <data key="d0">Soil Loss</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Soil Loss is the removal of soil particles by water, which is reduced by phosphogypsum application and improved soil structure.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dispersion Process">
  <data key="d0">Dispersion Process</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Dispersion Process is the breakdown of soil aggregates due to low electrolyte concentration, which phosphogypsum inhibits.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Flocculation">
  <data key="d0">Flocculation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Flocculation is the clumping of soil particles promoted by calcium, leading to improved soil structure and reduced dispersion.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Raindrop Impact">
  <data key="d0">Raindrop Impact</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Raindrop Impact is a physical force that causes surface aggregate disintegration, which phosphogypsum can mitigate by acting as a mulch.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Broadcast Application">
  <data key="d0">Broadcast Application</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Broadcast Application is a method of applying phosphogypsum as a surface layer, considered highly effective for water use efficiency.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Slope Gradient">
  <data key="d0">Slope Gradient</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Slope Gradient is a topographical factor that influences runoff and soil loss, with phosphogypsum showing varying effectiveness across different gradients.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Capillary Rise">
  <data key="d0">Capillary Rise</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Capillary Rise is a process that returns gypsum to the soil surface during drying periods, contributing to the long-lasting effects of phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Alkaline Soils">
  <data key="d0">Alkaline Soils</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Alkaline soils are a type of soil whose pH is reduced by phosphogypsum application.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Saline Soils">
  <data key="d0">Saline Soils</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Saline soils are a type of soil treated with phosphogypsum to improve physical properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Saline-Sodic Soils">
  <data key="d0">Saline-Sodic Soils</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Saline-sodic soils are a type of soil treated with phosphogypsum to improve physical properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Soil Texture">
  <data key="d0">Soil Texture</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Soil texture is a physical property that affects the extent of sodium leaching.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydraulic Conductivity">
  <data key="d0">Hydraulic Conductivity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A physical property of phosphogypsum indicating its ability to transmit water.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydraulic conductivity is a soil property that influences the extent of sodium leaching.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Water Use Efficiency">
  <data key="d0">Water Use Efficiency</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A metric evaluated in the context of phosphogypsum application rates and soil amendment effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Water use efficiency is a measure of agricultural productivity that is improved by broadcast application of phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-031</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cumulative Infiltration">
  <data key="d0">Cumulative Infiltration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Cumulative infiltration is the total amount of water entering the soil, increased by higher rates of phosphogypsum application.&lt;SEP&gt;A soil hydrological metric that was greatest with a specific phosphogypsum broadcast rate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-031</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Final Infiltration Rate">
  <data key="d0">Final Infiltration Rate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Final infiltration rate is the steady-state water entry rate into soil, increased by higher rates of phosphogypsum application.&lt;SEP&gt;A soil hydrological metric improved by phosphogypsum broadcast application.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-031</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Control Treatments">
  <data key="d0">Control Treatments</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Control treatments are untreated soil plots used as a baseline to measure the effectiveness of phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rainfall Impact Energy">
  <data key="d0">Rainfall Impact Energy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Rainfall impact energy is a factor that influences the relative effectiveness of phosphogypsum in reducing erosion.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rainfall Amount">
  <data key="d0">Rainfall Amount</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Rainfall amount is a factor that influences the capability of phosphogypsum to supply electrolytes during storms.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rainfall Intensity">
  <data key="d0">Rainfall Intensity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Rainfall intensity is a factor that influences the capability of phosphogypsum to supply electrolytes during storms.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rainfall Duration">
  <data key="d0">Rainfall Duration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Rainfall duration is a factor that influences the capability of phosphogypsum to supply electrolytes during storms.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030</data>
