<node id="Dihydrate Method">
  <data key="d0">Dihydrate Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The dihydrate method is a wet phosphoric acid process resulting in a uranium content of 5-25 μg/g in phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uranous Ion">
  <data key="d0">Uranous Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The uranous ion (U4+) forms complexes with fluoride under reduced conditions and low pH.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uranyl Ion">
  <data key="d0">Uranyl Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The uranyl ion is the chemical form of U(VI) adsorbed onto apatite crystal surfaces.&lt;SEP&gt;The uranyl ion (UO2 2+) dominates at pH below 5 and may adsorb onto gypsum surfaces or substitute calcium ions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Iron(II)">
  <data key="d0">Iron(II)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Iron(II) is a chemical species that reduces U(VI) to U(IV) during acidulation, facilitating uranium transfer to phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ferric Hydroxides">
  <data key="d0">Ferric Hydroxides</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Ferric hydroxides are environmental minerals that sorb uranium, following organics in sorption affinity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Clays">
  <data key="d0">Clays</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Clays are environmental minerals that sorb uranium, exhibiting lower sorption affinity than ferric hydroxides and oxyhydroxides.&lt;SEP&gt;Clays are materials that adsorb lead, keeping solution concentrations low.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pore Water">
  <data key="d0">Pore Water</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Pore water is a medium where ejected Th-234 atoms accumulate during radioactive decay processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Pore water is the water residing within the pore spaces of mineral structures, affecting Rn-222 transport and energy.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uranyl Fluoride">
  <data key="d0">Uranyl Fluoride</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Uranyl fluoride is a chemical species that dominates at pH below 5.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uranyl Carbonate">
  <data key="d0">Uranyl Carbonate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Uranyl carbonate species become prevalent at higher pH levels.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="UO2HPO4">
  <data key="d0">UO2HPO4</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">UO2HPO4 is a chemical compound formed when uranium(VI) adsorbs onto the surface of gypsum crystals.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="UO2(HPO4)2 2+">
  <data key="d0">UO2(HPO4)2 2+</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">UO2(HPO4)2 2+ is a chemical species that becomes prevalent between pH 4 and 7.5.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphorites">
  <data key="d0">Phosphorites</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphorites are sedimentary phosphate deposits that represent approximately 85% of known phosphate rock reserves.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Africa">
  <data key="d0">Africa</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Africa is a geographic region where the hemihydrate process for phosphoric acid production is relatively widely used.</data>
