<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Gypsum">
  <data key="d7">17.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between Gypsum and Phosphogypsum centers on their shared chemical composition, industrial formation, and distinct applications. Phosphogypsum is an industrial byproduct and waste material that is primarily composed of gypsum, which serves as its main calcium sulfate compound and mineral component. In many industrial contexts, gypsum is effectively the chemical name for this byproduct, though the two are distinguished by purity and processing stages. Gypsum directly contributes to the formation of the final phosphogypsum waste product. During extraction and purification treatments, particularly within Rare Earth Element recovery processes, the gypsum matrix within phosphogypsum undergoes recrystallization, purification, and recovery. Additionally, gypsum itself is generated as a byproduct during these purification treatments.

Despite their chemical overlap, Gypsum and Phosphogypsum serve distinct industrial applications. Phosphogypsum, which contains the purified gypsum matrix, is commonly utilized in soil amendments and building materials. Conversely, pure gypsum is valued as a sole calcium oxide source in cement production, where it can either substitute or complement phosphogypsum. This dual utility highlights how the same fundamental calcium sulfate compound is processed and deployed across different manufacturing sectors, with phosphogypsum representing the recycled or waste-derived form and gypsum serving as the refined or directly extracted material.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains gypsum, which is characterized by elongated, prismatic crystals.&lt;SEP&gt;Gypsum is used as a sole calcium oxide source in cement production, substituting or complementing Phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum is primarily composed of gypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Gypsum constitutes over 90 percent of the chemical composition of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Gypsum is the main crystal structure in phosphogypsum, allowing trace elements to substitute into its lattice.</data>
  <data key="d9">chemical composition,chemical equivalence,co-product,component,composition,contains,contribution,identification,industrial naming,matrix composition,primary component,primary composition,primary constituent,purification output,raw material,structural substitution,substitution,synonym,utilization,waste formation</data>
