<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="China">
  <data key="d7">9.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relationship between China and phosphogypsum is characterized by massive industrial output and ongoing environmental management efforts. China stands as the world's largest producer of phosphogypsum byproducts, generating and emitting approximately 80 million metric tons of the material annually. As a primary source region, the country serves as a major geographic focus for the extensive analysis and documentation of phosphogypsum production, disposal, generation, and utilization strategies.

To manage this substantial output, China employs specific storage conditions for the accumulated material. Despite these measures, current phosphogypsum management practices in the country face a significant gap in achieving zero-emission goals. In response to these challenges, Chinese authorities have established a formal policy target to achieve a 75% comprehensive utilization rate for phosphogypsum by 2025.</data>
  <data key="d9">emission,geographic source,location,management gap,policy,primary study region,production location,production statistics,production volume,storage,utilization context,utilization target</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d11">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d12">1791209577</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Rare Earth Elements">
  <data key="d7">30.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation (Phosphogypsum, Rare Earth Elements) describes the extraction and recovery of rare earth elements from phosphogypsum, a solid industrial byproduct generated during phosphoric acid production. During this manufacturing process, approximately 60% to 80% of the rare earth elements originally present in phosphate rock partition into the phosphogypsum waste stream. Within this matrix, the elements exist in multiple distinct states, including mineral inclusions, soluble salts, undissolved forms, and primarily through isomorphous substitution. These elements co-precipitate, become encapsulated, and are homogeneously dispersed across the material.

Phosphogypsum functions as both a primary source material and a valuable secondary, sustainable resource for rare earth element extraction. Specific deposits, such as phosphogypsum sourced from the Philippines, contain recoverable rare earth elements. The recovery process treats the material as a starting feedstock subjected to a sequenced chemical treatment regimen. Specialized leaching techniques, particularly using sulfuric acid solutions, systematically extract the embedded elements, followed by enrichment and purification stages.

Targeting these embedded rare earth elements for recovery offers significant dual benefits. It reduces the consumption of virgin natural resources and mitigates environmental pollution associated with the accumulation of industrial waste. Consequently, phosphogypsum is increasingly recognized as a viable raw material and primary matrix for sustainable resource management, effectively transforming an industrial byproduct into a critical mineral feedstock.&lt;SEP&gt;Rare earth elements are targeted for extraction and recovery from phosphogypsum waste streams.</data>
