<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Heavy Metals">
  <data key="d7">14.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Heavy Metals and Phosphogypsum characterize the presence of heavy metals as inherent, harmful contaminants within phosphogypsum, often occurring in trace amounts. Alongside radioactive elements, these heavy metals significantly influence the material's environmental behavior and overall safety profile. Due to their high solubility, heavy metals readily leach, diffuse, and permeate from phosphogypsum into the surrounding environment, a process that is particularly pronounced during long-term storage and decomposition. Consequently, phosphogypsum storage piles are frequently associated with elevated heavy metal concentrations in adjacent soils, where the contaminants accumulate over time. Furthermore, these leached metals can permeate into nearby water bodies, posing substantial risks to ecological systems and human health.

Given the environmental and health hazards posed by these contaminants, specific management protocols are required to ensure ecological safety. The heavy metals and radioactive elements embedded within phosphogypsum necessitate targeted stabilization and passivation treatments. These interventions are essential to immobilize the contaminants, prevent their further migration into soil and water systems, and mitigate the long-term environmental impact of phosphogypsum disposal and storage.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains heavy metals as harmful components that require stabilization.</data>
  <data key="d9">component,composition,contains,contains harmful component,contaminant,contamination,contamination risk,content,environmental association,environmental concern,environmental hazard,leaching,pollution,releases</data>
